('hammer-pair-01', 1, 'primary', 74, 'rwondb', 'AITA for telling my husband we shouldn’t have paid for his 60th BD party his sister suggested throwing for him?', 'In October my sister-in-law asked my husband if he wanted to have a 60th Bday party in his hometown, which is several states away, and requires us to fly back for. He asked me what I thought, and I said if you want it, I will go. (I don’t enjoy his family, but after 30+ years I can put up with them for a long weekend) 

Between October and December, it went from a single party to a dinner on Friday at her house with 10 of his catholic HS friends and their wives, and a party at a local bar on Saturday with around 45 people, friends and his family(many the same as the night before) wives included. It would have been more but one of his brothers and his kids had COVID from thanksgiving. 

I was getting more and more uncomfortable, especially with Covid and all, but I have learned to STFU over the years. I mentally started calling his 60th his Quinceañera, at this point. She started including me on food choices, which I resisted as long as I could, after all, this wasn’t my f#€king idea, and I wanted no ownership over it. 

We made reservations for the plane tickets, and I was becoming increasingly bitter, as the cheapest flights were Spirit Airlines, and I swore I would never travel them again after a horrible flt back in 2019, where I flew from LAX to ATL. (easily the worst and longest  flt I have ever taken. )

Fast forward to early Dec, and the long weekend of spending time with a bunch of 60 yo prep school guys and their brittle blonde wives..I was the DD all weekend back to the hotel, which was fine. It gave me something to do and occupied me pouring the wine and making sure everyone was having a nice time. Alcohol would not have made me a better person at this point, and I made the right choice for all involved to not loosen any inhibitions and make me moody or a smart a$$.

Fast forward to Saturday night and the bar party..I mingled, drinking my tonic and lime, talking to people I would never see again, even though I kept promising to get dinner with them every time they flew or drove through Atlanta (knowing I will plan to not be in town when this happens..🤫). At around 10:30, I asked my SIL if she thought we should think about wrapping it up since everyone was getting pretty sloppy and I was tired. She said you can settle with the person at the bar..🙄🤨..

SHE STUCK ME WITH THE TAB FOR THE PARTY SHE SUGGESTED IN THE FIRST PLACE. The tab was almost $2,000, on top of the hotel $675, and the food for the lunch that day for all the guys and their wives who traveled to Cleveland for the weekend $300. Plus airfare, $200, which being Spirit, at least that was cheap. 

Am I the asshole here for telling my husband and my kids that she should have covered the party that she suggested to him??? Am I an asshole for being very cold to her, and still angry that I am out $3k and I didn’t even have fun?', 'cba5970debc23f35cad2c53a2e9cb1679f70b758f1a1e995b491f841c025e8ae', '17qufl5', 'AITA for opting out of Christmas gifts or suggesting switching to the "Secret Santa" format?', 'I have a 4 siblings, 2 parents, 1 niece and one nephew and one brother "Jack" who just got married to a woman with 3 kids. Prior to this year, we''ve always gotten presents for each other. Christmas always gets way too expensive, especially since I have my own romantic partner to shop for as well. 

In our family groupchat my brother  who just got married to us all he just wants to set the expectation that his step kids are equal parts of the family and he expects us to get them gifts this year as well. From a moral standpoint- fair enough I guess- but that just makes this even more expensive. Its been slowly getting more expensive as my niece and nephew get older but its kinda fun to get them presents and at least that was slowly building. For this, they''re nice kids but I dont feel love for them and its just 3 new ones all at once. I suggested this year we just switch to a secret santa format, pull names and each just get a gift for someone plus parents can get stuff for their own kids.

One of my siblings chimed in saying we should do that as well, the 3 extra gifts were just too much for him, and he agrees its gotten out of hand. Well Jack took that as an opportunity to say were being assholes to his kids by excluding them. I said we would keep them in the Secret Santa group so they wouldnt be excluded, I just didnt want to spend another say $60 on them. 

Its caused a hole thing where im getting called cheap by some of them and an AH by Jack. I just feel like its making christmas more if a stressor than a fun holiday and at this point Id like to just opt out. ', 'b9013bd4d5fdbd6729a3506bb017d007618445d1a5bddd2c295b668027cfcbff', '["NTA but you should have immediately handed the tab to your husband and told him his sister was refusing to pay. My suspicion is that your husband knew all along he was paying, but didn''t want to tell you because you''d make him cancel. SIL might not be an asshole at all and thought you knew you were paying.\n\nLate edit, can''t stop thinking about this: Also, any guest who expects the birthday celebrant to pay for their entire bar tab, the night before their birthday, without ever checking with them is also an asshole. Who told these people it was an open bar?","INFO: do you as in you and your husband pay for it with joint funds? Or did you pay for it from your personal funds?\n\nI''d say the silver lining is you''ve got a great excuse to nope out of the next family gathering. \"gee, I''d like to come but I didn''t plan the two grand for hubby''s bday so we don''t have the funds to visit.\"","NTA. Whatever happened to showing up to these places and paying your own way? If I invite guests to my home, yes, I''ll pay for the drinks and dinner. If I ask people to meet me at the pub, I''d expect they pay their own tab. Did the sister tell all these people \"don''t worry, we will pay\" and not communicate that with you? I''d be pissed too.","YTA for refusing to have any fun and seeing the absolute worst version of every part of this. \"Brittle blonde wives\" okay??? You sound so absolutely pointlessly cynical and negative it''s literally painful to read your thoughts, how do you stand to have them?\n\nNTA for being mad about the check, I agree with whoever said you should hand it to your husband and tell him your sister is refusing to pay. Totally fair to be mad about being blindsided.","ESH. Your sister-in-law should have never expected you to pay. But you spent this entire post talking about yourself, about how you were uncomfortable, about how you didn''t want to spend time with these people, about how you didn''t have fun. News flash OP, it was a party for your husband. Did he feel comfortable, did he want to spend time with these people, did he have fun? If the answer is yes, then at the end of the day, it was worth it."]'::jsonb, '["33fdabf79b5411d3f44be4d969c37d5ec0e05c50fa79855ef82bd7fad64831af","b442870872e4d64133525ade317227b56d7256a39f9cb32c3f0f0322c3ae1a4f","03d8d0533242c41256bad301d6d1b6b44cb24bbfd5edaddcfe61fb271054dacc","13e50ee12931bc60abb833cecd07223c793fb56edcc6c2ecc142c5075878dd0d","c729a231ad9662dd1f3ebc18c171ffc0757efc5c7a35c61fe0d18dc1b1c451d1"]'::jsonb, '["YTA\n\nYou agreed to attend this party and participated in the planning, even reluctantly. While your SIL''s actions in sticking you with the tab are incredibly rude and presumptuous, your husband is the primary person who should have handled this situation. The financial responsibility for his own birthday party should have been discussed and clarified with his sister beforehand, and this is a failure in communication between the two of them. Your anger is misdirected; you should be holding your husband accountable for not managing his family and the expectations for his event. Being cold to your SIL and complaining to your kids doesn''t address the core issue, which is a lack of clear planning and agreement between the siblings.","YTA\n\nThe sister-in-law suggested the party, but she never offered to foot the bill. By assuming she would pay and then getting angry and cold when she didn''t, you''re treating the costs as her responsibility even though they were yours to negotiate up front. It was your and your husband''s decision to attend and spend the money, so it''s unreasonable to demand she cover it after the fact. Your frustration is understandable, but the way you''re handling it - especially the cold treatment and blaming her for a cost you never clarified - makes you the asshole here.","YTA\n\nThe user''s sister-in-law did suggest the party, but it was the husband who agreed to it, and the user also agreed to attend, saying \"if you want it, I will go.\" The user knew from the start that they wouldn''t enjoy the party, but still chose to go, and even participated in it by being the designated driver and mingling with the guests. While it''s understandable that the user is upset about being stuck with the tab, it''s not entirely clear that the sister-in-law explicitly agreed to cover the costs, and the user didn''t discuss or clarify the financial arrangements beforehand. The user''s attitude and behavior towards the sister-in-law, being cold and angry, seems unreasonable given the circumstances, and they could have handled the situation more constructively by discussing the costs with their husband or sister-in-law before the party.","NTA\n\nYour sister-in-law''s suggestion to throw your husband a party turned into a massive, expensive event that you never agreed to organize or fund. Being stuck with a nearly $2,000 tab-on top of thousands in other costs-with no prior discussion is completely unreasonable. You supported your husband by attending despite your discomfort, but being suddenly made responsible for the financial burden she created crosses a major line. Your frustration is completely valid, and you''re not the asshole for being angry over this situation.","NTA\n\nYou were not responsible for organizing or funding the event, and your sister-in-law overstepped by placing the financial burden on you without prior discussion. You supported your husband by attending an event you were uncomfortable with, even serving as the designated driver and managing logistics. Being upset about being unexpectedly stuck with a $2,000 bar tab-especially for a party she proposed and orchestrated-is completely reasonable. Holding her accountable doesn''t make you the asshole; it makes you justified in setting boundaries. Your frustration over spending nearly $3,000 on an event you didn''t want, didn''t plan, and didn''t enjoy is valid. NTA."]'::jsonb, '["e15713574c22dd35f8232f64720ed577ccf8aaa7969a461d8c8ab743e7ad69ca","e29e87a66f35982eddd9a5dc4aafd0a1ff4a031d26ba7d982c21bd6c676ac0ff","1c1c627bd1b9b3fbf6186cf826a528123e6b6e8d9a354ccd5eedbd8eb6c03892","138e821c0b9c8aca04eccd024a9cbeac485aaeef0e24733f23e4f7e71002a155","caf75bf0a12843f6b3039211a92968b1561dc635cf2297b92b9e3c5780a28dc3"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"家人能否在未事先取得同意时把庆典相关的金钱义务强加给他人，以及被要求者能否拒付或改变制度","surfaceDifference":"A 是小姑安排丈夫生日后让夫妻承担账单；B 是兄弟要求全家临时增加圣诞礼物，OP 提议改为 Secret Santa","directAnalogyRisk":"low"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-02', 2, 'primary', 25, '1ebuqfo', 'AITA for getting mad over the fact that my boyfriend went to the birthday of a friend but not mine', 'I, 19 F, invited my boyfriend, 19 M (soon 20 M) to my birthday. This event was important to me because, well, it was my birthday, but it was also the first time since elementary school that I organized something with friends since I grew up pretty lonely. 

My boyfriend did not go to my birthday, not because he had something more important planned that day, he was completely free, but because he did not want to meet my friends, especially a guy friend he is jealous of and do not like, even if he had never met him, because he knows that he criticized him. 

I asked him several times if he really would not come because I really wanted him to, as this little party was important to me and I love him so I wanted him to be there. 
He insisted on the fact that he would not come.

I knew that the next day, a friend of his also celebrated his birthday. But he told me that he would not come because it would be disrespectul as he is not coming to my birthday and he was not really motivated to go. 
But the day after my birthday, he told me that he would go. It was a surprise party and the gf of his friend thought that there would be only 3 person so she begged him to come. So he did, bringing three friends of his so there would be more people. I was invited too so I came. Actually, even without him and the friends he brought, there would have been enough people. But the gf did not know that. 
I was really mad because he went to this birthday and not mine even though I am his girlfriend so I am supposed to be more important. He told me that if there were gonna be only 3 people at my party he would have come, that the circumptances were exceptional, that he would have felt uncomfortable around my friends and that l was allowed to not come to his birthday (later this year) for the same reason. But he is my boyfriend, even if I feel uncomfortable around his friends which will probably be the case since i have jealousy issues (I am working on them), I will come to his birthday because it''s important. He understood why I was upset but he also told me to understand his pov. Honestly I can''t because I would have not acted that way, plus a friend of mine who has social anxiety made the effort to come to my birthday. But maybe it''s just me ? AITA for not understanding his pov and getting mad about this ?', '699475d7e3cb7d1aaaafd79cc3ef3054c33077236eb2d9cc68934237d5dccbf2', 'queqy4', 'AITA for going home after my boyfriend and his family kept me waiting outside his room at the hospital?', 'My f29 boyfriend m32 of 5 years had an accident days ago and was admitted to the hospital. He has a knee injury nothing too serious. On his 3rd day at the hospital I got off work and went to visit him.


When I arrived his family was there too. I was told to go stand outside of the room by his family after I entered the room. I couldn''t even speak to him and he was 100% awake and saw me.

I was standing there in the hallway with them completely ignoring me for 3hrs straight.
After waiting for that long I just walked out without saying a word since I didn''t even think they acknowledged my presence. I went home feeling upset and his mom called to scold me for leaving like that. When I opened up to her about how felt I was constantly ignored and how felt like my presence wasn''t important since they told me to wait outside she was shocked telling me this isn''t about me my boyfriend just got out of a horrible situation and I should be happy and feel grateful for getting him back instead of sulking and craving attention.

She explained that my boyfriend was on medication and went on about how me leaving like that painted me in a bad light. She shamed saying I should feel ashamed of myself for not giving my boyfriend the support he obviously needed and brought an overall negative energy to the rest of the worried family members. She made me feel absolutely guilty even though she was the one who requested I wait outside but I''m not sure wether I did the right thing by leaving after waiting for 3hr straight.

Was I in the wrong for leaving? Should I have waited a bit longer?', '7aa09013a5d899d7a54e2e3abd0ec1818133fa4f703312472ad58bcaebd018b3', '["YTA\n\nFor tolerating this bullshit. Stop trying to make it about his friend''s birthday party.\n\nYour boyfriend is treating you poorly. He doesn''t care about your feelings and is willingly hurtful. The fact that he would take steps to hurt you on your birthday is just the rotten cherry on top of a shit sundae.\n\nHe''s punishing you for having friends. He won''t spend time doing things with you and your friends, even on your birthday.\n\nThe day after being disrespected, you go along with whatever he wants to do.","nta\n\nthe problem''s not that he went to the other birthday, but that he didn''t come to yours\n\nsounds like there''s lack of communication between you two, bcs there''s this problem of your friend he''s so jealous of that he won''t even join his girls birthday party","INFO: If your boyfriend has never met your friend, how does he know that the guy criticized him?\n\nI''m leaning towards N-T-A because your boyfriend''s reasons for not going to your party seem rather selfish and hateful - it doesn''t matter if he doesn''t like your friends, he''s meant to be there for you, not them and not himself; and having been someone like you who didn''t have a lot of people celebrating me on birthdays, I totally understand why it would mean so much to you to have him there.\n\nBUT . . . if you''re going around repeating to your boyfriend shitty things that your other friends are saying about him, then it''s E-S-H. That''s a really awful thing to do. He doesn''t need to hear, \"Oh, my friend that you haven''t even met is criticizing you!\" I hope you''re not doing that.","ESH OP - he''s not comfortable with your guy friend who criticised him. Without fully understanding that situation, I can''t judge but it is clear your BF feels strongly about it.\n\nWe don''t force people to be in the company of those that are toxic to them so I''d say you need to think through this clearly. Berating him and makimg an issue of it with BF will get you nowhere fast. If this is something your BF can''t move past or feels strongly about that he can''t see himsekf ever being around this guy, you might need to make a choice.","NTA. Your post had me rolling my eyes the whole time. I know you won''t do it because you sound head over heels for your shitty bf but the right thing to do would to break up with his ass for being a shitty BF. Sounds like even some of your friends know it.\n\nAnyway I doubt you''ll do it but hopefully these comments will make you realize hes shitty and at best he needs to change to keep you. Yall are young, don''t waste your time on shitty guys.\n\nWhen you do breakup come back to these comments and let us know. From an outsiders perspective I think one day you''ll fully realize he''s shitty when you''ve had enough and leave him. Might not feel that way rn but very high likelihood if he continues being an asshat."]'::jsonb, '["99dd0b5c0a2665e7eacabe532c22fb87d04d52f91fe81ec6ceabab3cae2d28f4","70edeee153aad077c463657cac1db27accb34b6f1efbf2bc4aa647444521eef3","89cee36b24f0c8af150107e6f8e78e3306badc04da0d3d8d3440b3512872b256","4230c2f4f773cbe4322ad7074fcecc4ef5a90604889520b981230011036e8502","fd37260f2f432dcc087011f50d045f201ed04e6033b26193e0fe49fd9a6ac9cf"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYour boyfriend prioritized his own comfort and jealousy over a milestone event that was important to you. His excuse that he would have come if there were only three people is irrelevant, as that was not the case for your party. The fact that he then attended his friend''s party, even after initially claiming he wouldn''t, demonstrates a clear double standard and a lack of consideration for your feelings. While his point about feeling uncomfortable is valid, a supportive partner would make an effort for a significant occasion, especially when you explicitly asked him to be there. His actions show a lack of prioritization for your relationship.","NTA\n\nYour boyfriend chose to skip your birthday for personal jealousy and discomfort, yet later attended another friend''s party after previously saying he wouldn''t-showing a double standard and a lack of consideration for an event that was clearly important to you. It''s reasonable for you to feel upset and to want him to understand his actions, especially when you''d still attend his birthday despite any discomfort. Your frustration isn''t unjustified, so you''re not the asshole here.","YTA\n\nYour boyfriend''s decision not to attend your birthday party because he was uncomfortable around your friends, especially one he''s jealous of, is understandable to some extent. However, your anger towards him for attending his friend''s birthday party afterwards is also valid given the circumstances. Nevertheless, your expectation that he should prioritize your feelings and birthday over his own discomfort, and your comparison to how you would act in a similar situation, overlooks the fact that people have different boundaries and comfort levels in social situations. By not considering his perspective and instead focusing solely on your own feelings and expectations, you''re not fully acknowledging his reasons for not attending your party. Furthermore, his willingness to attend his friend''s party due to exceptional circumstances (the belief that there would only be three people) doesn''t necessarily negate his initial decision regarding your party. Your demand for understanding while not fully reciprocating it places you in a position where you''re not entirely open to his point of view, hence making you the asshole in this scenario.","YTA\nYour boyfriend''s reasoning is consistent, even if it stings. He didn''t skip your birthday because he doesn''t care; he skipped because of his discomfort around a specific friend and admitted that av openly. When his friend''s GF tapped him to save her party, he had a clear, time-sensitive reason to go-one that didn''t involve facing the same jealousy issue. You comparing your own efforts (like your socially anxious friend attending) ignores that he flagged his boundaries upfront and even offered you the same freedom for his birthday. Relationships should prioritize mutual understanding, not scoreboard logic. He communicated clearly; you''re hurt, but his actions don''t make him an asshole.","NTA\n\nYou''re not the asshole for being upset. Your birthday was clearly important to you, especially as a milestone moment after feeling isolated growing up. You asked your boyfriend to attend, and he refused-not due to a scheduling conflict or prior commitment, but because of unfounded jealousy toward a friend he''s never even met. That''s a personal choice rooted in insecurity, not a valid excuse. Meanwhile, he ended up attending his friend''s birthday, even going so far as to bring three extra people to \"help\" with guest numbers, which contradicts his claim that he wouldn''t attend your event for similar reasons. His argument that you wouldn''t be obligated to attend his birthday if you felt uncomfortable doesn''t hold weight-this is about reciprocity and respect in a relationship. You made it clear how much you wanted him there; he prioritized someone else''s feelings over yours. While it''s understandable he might feel uneasy, his refusal to support you on such an important day-while showing up for others-justifies your hurt and anger. You have every right to expect basic support from a partner, especially when you''ve extended that support in return."]'::jsonb, '["91379e83a179789066a12ccd61ded2736e5c244fab00c1bf6dd308ef44ad9833","4bd0836215662953b6938208d2700cf892efd6567c6571cc11f6d901bcaa5514","05fe82c049d0c6343b4ad2d998455c8bf68bc0ba4fa868da7f660a19fa930b1a","bc5cf5f74cfda182c514f57aa032aec2095fa64cc1bb3a2db8cd988250623781","47b2b00557efb2066c7a44ba0ed6bc0b3d66e3876f2b804855fc8b52697017c1"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"伴侣在重要或脆弱时刻是否有到场和支持义务，以及这种义务何时会被不适或排斥抵消","surfaceDifference":"A 是男友因不愿见朋友而缺席生日；B 是女友在医院被家人排除并等待三小时后离开","directAnalogyRisk":"medium"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-03', 3, 'primary', 54, '1hivqx2', 'AITA for being offended that I have to sign a contract on who’s gonna play the music for a road trip?', 'As I’m moving 8 hours away from where I currently live, my long-distance boyfriend (24M) agreed to help me (25F) move and will be driving the car. 
I really appreciate it that he will come from 8h away for a weekend and help me move, especially during his exam season (he has one easy exam after the move, so the trip won’t be really damaging for his studying).

However, during our last call, he said that we will have to agree that out of 8h trip, he will get to play the music majority of the ride (5h) while I get the rest. This idea of splitting the music time came from our last road trip together when he was driving as well, and believed that as he is the driver, gets choose the music the whole trip as that was his dad’s rule his whole life. I however come from a family where we either listened to radio, or to music we all generally like. And that is my logic too - we can mix up songs here and there and listen to “our” music instead of one person having the complete power on music choice. During that trip, we kinda bickered around but generally mixed it up till a point where I accidentally cut off his song by instead of adding the song to que, played it right away where he got offended. I apologized as that was not my intention, but understood it why it was disrespectful for him as I would probably feel the same. At that point, I also tried explaining to him that I like to play music not only for my own enjoyment, but also as a way to share my likes with him and show him perhaps new music that he might like. I of course play a lot lf songs that I know we both like anyway. For him, it’s not do enjoyable as he is generally not open to new music. But we generally got over it and now just joke about it.



I understand that because of the past incident, he thinks that it would again be the case even though I reassured him that it wouldn’t be the case. But now, he actually sent me “a contract” he wants me to sign consenting that he will play the music for majority of time (5h out of 8h).

I generally don’t mind that that much especially as the songs he played I also like, and in the end he is doing me a big favor. However, I can’t help but feel like his help is somehow conditional? And like he doesn’t believe me if I just say that is fine.  

To be honest, I just feel like my likes and my enjoyment are not being appreciated, and I tried communicating this. But however, I might also totally be the asshole.
', 'dc3ae88c8979b673bfaf405473b58838ad622b352ee18b38b561c13e9c357d58', 'ejlw3r', 'AITA for not wanting SO to listen to podcasts all the time?', 'My SO is a big fan of podcasts. He listens to at least 10 and has them downloaded to his phone. I am not a big fan of podcasts because voices distract me a lot. I prefer to listen to music or nothing at all, and on the occasions when I do listen to a podcast, I don''t do many episodes because I get headaches.  


My problem -and the reason why I think I might be the asshole- is because he is constantly listening to podcasts all the time. I don''t mind the subject, the guys or whatever the joke is about. What I mind is that he puts them on his phone, all volume up and it''s just one after the other on loop for hours. There''s been times when I''ve found his phone on the kitchen with a podcast, his computer on with ANOTHER podcast and the TV on with another one. Hearing all this voices all the time stresses me and I get to the point where I just tell him to shut them up. He has stopped listening to a few because of this and I feel awful about it because it''s not that I don''t want him to listen to them... I just wish I didn''t have to hear them 12 hours a day as well.

Recently I told this to my brothers, and they said I was overexaggerating and being mean, but it really bugs me. Can''t he just headphones?

Am I the asshole?', '9c2beb1cac9943db67fc32392647b500f0007cef53fe0a1fc8806ec5dee048b2', '["YTA\n\nIf I''m driving my car, I''m in charge of the music 100% of the time.\n\nIf I''m in someone else''s car, I don''t say a word about their music unless it''s so awful that I want to jump out the window.\nIn which case, I ask, can we listen to literally anything else?","YTA\n\nBabe, read the room. He is literally coming from over 8 hours away to help YOU move, this isn''t a romantic weekend. And you''re getting pissy that he wants 5 hours of his music, and the other 3 hours will be what you wants? I would take that deal if someone was helping me move. Also hope you''re buying him whatever lunch he wants.","NAH. I think you are wrong, but don''t think this rises to level of AH on anyones part. Your incessance on this is probably why he wants it clear that you are agreeing.\n\nMy big thing is: why don''t you consider listening to his music being his way of sharing his likes and perhaps new music you might like?\n\nWhy is him playing his music ONLY for him but you playing your music for BOTH of you?","YTA.\n\nI drove with someone who wanted to listen to NPR the whole time. Even when I was driving, it was just NPR. And it put me to fucking sleep. I could not keep my eyes open and told them they would have to finish driving or we would have to find somewhere to nap.\n\nI need some thing with lively music, some thing I can sing too. I need car karaoke. I need to be having conversations with people or listening to exciting things.\n\nI seriously preferred when I was listening to a game on the radio while driving and a podcast about farmland, cause at least everyone was cheering and there was stuff going on. NPR has such even tones it makes me want to fall asleep. It''s like someone is singing me a lullaby.\n\nSo, when someone is driving they get to pick the music because they know what keeps them awake and aware. They are driving.","INFO: What does it say in the relationship contract about playlist selection?\n\nSeriously I think you have taken this argument way into the weeds and you are both approaching it in the most ungracious, ungenerous, contentious way possible and it doesn''t bode well for the relationship that you didn''t see this as just a fun opportunity to be together and create a mutual playlist."]'::jsonb, '["1b0870f66deec71a92d7653403e3049adf0ca5c8d8e842fa683130332274ce28","adb9336d9dc4d6556dcaf6ad173cec4030b0c52aef402cbdf62a9ff2cedbb406","c015125c69205a6f94a1581907ca1d7b419933cbdfa761de72bcaee676855cf8","eae0ec4969d51c0b1f76a3ce26aeccd51b5fb45f4d157b769a2540617b651a60","6ef19f4da56ab94356a2fde3b69ae3b117bc0b02a518f22dc7a124353a702dac"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nWhile your boyfriend is doing you a favor, a healthy partnership is built on mutual respect and compromise, not rigid contracts and conditional help. His \"driver picks the music\" rule is arbitrary, and his insistence on a formal agreement over a simple road trip playlist is controlling and dismissive of your feelings. You''ve apologized for the past mistake and communicated your desire to share music with him, which is a normal and positive part of a relationship. His refusal to trust your word and his need to codify his musical dominance for a majority of the trip makes his help feel transactional rather than supportive.","NTA\n\nThe request to sign a \"contract\" dictating who gets to control the music for most of an 8-hour drive is unreasonable and overly controlling, especially given that he''s already doing you a huge favor by traveling that far to help you move. Music preferences are a minor, negotiable detail on a road trip, and it''s perfectly reasonable for you to want a more balanced share or to mix both of your tastes. His insistence that he must dominate the playlist-especially in writing-makes the help feel conditional and diminishes your enjoyment. You''ve already apologized for the past misunderstanding and tried to communicate your perspective, so you''re not being unreasonable or selfish. The boyfriend''s demand is the problematic behavior here.","NTA\n\nYour boyfriend is being overly controlling and insecure about the music situation, which is understandable given the past incident, but his request for a \"contract\" comes across as petty and conditional. You''ve already reassured him that you''re willing to compromise and listen to his music, and it''s reasonable to expect that he would trust you and respect your wishes as well. His behavior suggests that he''s not valuing your feelings or opinions, and instead is trying to exert control over the situation. While you do appreciate his help with the move, it''s not unreasonable to feel that his help is being offered conditionally, and that your own enjoyment and preferences are being dismissed.","NTA\n\nYour boyfriend''s need to turn a music preference into a legally styled contract is a massive overreaction and infantilizes both of you. While his help with the move is appreciated, it doesn''t grant him the right to control every aspect of the experience, especially something as trivial as music. Road trips are about shared experiences, and compromise is a natural part of that. Instead of trusting you after your previous conversation, he''s chosen to enforce rigid, arbitrary rules, which feels less about music and more about control. Your feelings of being unappreciated are valid, and this isn''t just about the playlist-it''s about respect and communication in your relationship.","NTA\n\nYou''re not the asshole for feeling hurt and disrespected by being asked to sign a \"contract\" over music rights during a road trip. While your boyfriend may be trying to prevent conflict based on a past incident, turning a minor issue into a formal agreement comes across as controlling and unnecessarily rigid. Compromise is reasonable-like splitting listening time or creating a shared playlist-but demanding a signed contract crosses a line into treating a relationship like a transaction. His actions suggest he values being \"right\" or \"in control\" over mutual trust and flexibility. You''ve already apologized for the previous incident and expressed your desire to share music as a bonding experience, which shows emotional maturity. His refusal to trust your word and insistence on a contract makes this about power, not fairness. That said, his effort to help you move is commendable, but it doesn''t justify making you feel invalidated. A healthy compromise doesn''t require paperwork."]'::jsonb, '["f83bf01516c8fcae5ea60a48cc9b473bd1d6a97f126df880b44bf01c4e13abc4","54db87c05ab618258a4762807e8b7ad0cf3fea29d5f7e18f284b8503f13eb469","b4bb9e80ce1a32e8ed754485e296768a2641e5dffda8a8e78d1d8b08798daffb","6e20263e8eebba189eae975e3aa4359358da3b6cdabdc99faf3289384ba6e567","2e79eba8f9352bb69ad823a1d4d2cc143a45a1d759a4d49ab7dc3ccaa6d11da5"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"共享环境中一方对媒体的偏好或控制权，如何与另一方的舒适、感官负担和合理迁就相平衡","surfaceDifference":"A 是搬家公路行程中的音乐时段协议；B 是同居空间全天外放播客以及是否使用耳机","directAnalogyRisk":"medium"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-04', 4, 'primary', 5, 'qqwbcx', 'AITA for getting back at my roommate?', 'When my new roommate, Dave, moved in a few months ago, we discussed a few ground rules for the apartment, one of which being no shoes inside. Dave reluctantly agreed saying that he has always worn shoes inside and it would take some time to change the habit. He tried it for the first few weeks but now completely gives up and keeps dragging his shoes all over the common area and even into his room (which I find it extremely gross but it’s his room so whatever). I tried talking to him but he wouldn’t budge, saying that he should be comfortable at his own home. This really upset me so I decided that if he doesn’t respect the initial agreement then neither should I. 

Dave used to complain about my cooking, especially the stronger smelling food like kimchi and fish sauce. I’ve refrained from using them but last week, I got exactly just that, some kimchi and fish sauce. I would be lying if I said I didn’t do it to annoy Dave but I do eat them regularly if not for him. To no surprise, he is pissed and complains that the entire apartment, the fridge and even his food is stinking. So I told him that I should be comfortable eating the food I like. Am I in the wrong here?', 'd5bafaedf33139a0c0ace97b0716f34875167c5714f881f75ee4a729882f755f', 'lpuqbg', 'AITA for wearing a tank top and shorts around my roommate’s boyfriend in my own home?', '
I (26F) just moved in with a roommate (31F). For the most part, we’ve gotten along and she’s even met a couple of my friends and we have meals together and shoot the shit.

Something about me: I gained some weight in quarantine, like many of us have. Most of this weight gain went to my chest. I don’t want to have to get new bras and I’ve been trying to lose the weight and get back to my old size. In the meantime, I’ve been using pasties and wearing oversized t shirts. 

My roommate has her boyfriend (31M) over a few times a week. There’s been no issues there. But this morning, my roommate raised an issue regarding how I dress in front of the boyfriend.

The dude was over yesterday, which I realized after I woke up from a very long nap. I was in a tank top and shorts when I went into the living room. I see them there and stay to chat like I normally do. Then the boyfriend went to the fridge to get a beer. I followed suit because I wanted one too and I was going to make myself a late dinner. 

So he handed me a beer and we were all just shooting the shit. I thought nothing of it until my roommate, at breakfast this morning (sans boyfriend), told me she was uncomfortable with me being in close proximity to her boyfriend in a tank top and shorts with no bra on.

I should say that no, my nipples were not visible and the tank top was not of the variant most of you are familiar with them being. I was decent around him and at an appropriate distance. She mentioned being insecure and traditional (she’s Asian), and asked if I could at least have a hoodie on in future, especially if I wasn’t going to wear a bra and her boyfriend was going to hand me things. 

I told her to stop tearing down other women and forcing them to cover up and work on her own issues. I pay rent to live here and also, not only am I not out to steal everyone’s man, I’m Hella gay. I don’t even like men. I told her I was going to wear what made me comfortable and to take it up with her boyfriend if she felt he was staring.

She said she wasn’t worried about me but just wanted to have just mutual respect, and called me defensive and that she knew I didn’t want her boyfriend but that I didn’t need to be so arrogant. But I don’t think I was being arrogant. I hate this weight gain and I’m trying to lose it. And I shouldn’t have to have my clothes policed when I don’t live with mommy and daddy anymore. AITA here?', 'f86045c6970d489f26c7f6d1f25d6dee6beb69417dd0712f3cd54ffc0b4738d1', '["NTA because wearing shoes inside the house is disgusting. Tell him that if he wants to wear shoes inside the house, he needs to get a new pair of shoes, which he will only use inside so that they won''t be dirty. That shouldn''t be a problem for either of you.","ESH\n\nDave should make more of an effort to do something you''ve both agreed on, but doing something specifically to annoy him puts you as an asshole here as well. You absolutely should be able to eat whatever you want (within reason, of course), but agreeing not to eat something and then doing it specifically to piss someone off is shitty","INFO: why not try to compromise, you can eat your kimchi and fish sauce if Dave wants to wear his shoes.\n\nAlso, I''m on Dave''s side just because I genuinely do not feel comfortable with shoes off in the house (I think it''s an anxiety thing), but I got crocs for house shoes that I don''t wear outside. Would you be comfortable with a pair of inside shoes?","ESH- talk about passive aggressive . Y''all are adults and should be able to talk about the situation like adults. It seems like neither of you are willing to communicate or compromise, so it is probably better/easier to find a new roommate.\n\nYou have to realize that there are cultural differences and you were each raised differently. While taking your shoes off may be logical to you, to this other person it is normal to wear shoes in the house. Y''all need to accept your differences and learn to be ok with it.","NTA - I wear shoes in my own home, but will take them off at friends houses. You BOTH agreed to some house rules when you moved in together and he decided they didn''t apply to him, the agreement was broken by him so you don''t need to uphold it either.\n\n(Anyone else find it a bit racist that OP was asked not to use certain asian foods? Fish sauce isn''t even that smelly)"]'::jsonb, '["8b614c6e13d3c79d80ea04b10c419ea231d96d1e5a9d4ffaa43a69c9bcd854ef","8ad584db34d378c0bacbd961b106ef59046ebdee6277da7289096960fb50b903","3bb6cec719a707e19eccb4c45e42fb0c61d6ece2852ffd296d13f9844d52ec07","b740223c24079fae9100a7ce23fd1788c758335f24abcbca33e68bd1b00f38f4","8e6bf6102cccd8ea05280daeaf21806ab2d411dfdd0180c93745fa428b89799b"]'::jsonb, '["YTA\n\nYou both agreed to a no-shoes rule, and Dave is breaking that agreement. However, your response wasn''t about addressing the broken agreement through communication or finding a compromise. Instead, you deliberately chose to retaliate by doing something you knew would upset him, using his own words against him. While you enjoy those foods, your primary motivation was to annoy him, which is an asshole move. Two wrongs don''t make a right, and this passive-aggressive behavior only escalates the conflict instead of resolving it.","ESH\n\nBoth of you are in the wrong. Dave broke the original agreement about shoes and should have respected the compromise you discussed. In response, you escalated the conflict by deliberately cooking strong-smelling foods to annoy him, which is a petty retaliation rather than a reasonable solution. Neither behavior is justified, and both of you need to communicate more constructively and respect each other''s boundaries.","YTA\n\nYou and your roommate initially agreed on a set of ground rules, one of which was no shoes inside, and he has since disregarded this agreement. However, instead of finding a constructive way to address the issue, such as reminding him of the agreement or finding a compromise, you decided to retaliate by deliberately cooking strong-smelling foods that you knew he disliked. This action was intended to annoy him, which makes your behavior equally disrespectful and petty. By doing so, you escalated the situation and created more tension, rather than resolving the issue or finding a mutually respectful solution.","ESH\nYou both agreed to the no-shoes rule, and Dave is breaking that agreement, which makes him an asshole for not respecting the shared space and your attempts to resolve the issue. However, your response of intentionally cooking pungent foods to \"get back at him\" is childish and escalates the conflict rather than solving it. You intentionally created a hostile environment in shared spaces, which makes you an asshole as well. Both of you are prioritizing your own comfort over mutual respect and compromise, making everyone suck here.","YTA\n\nYou agreed to a mutual living arrangement and, when your roommate broke one rule (no shoes indoors), you responded by intentionally violating another unspoken expectation (not deliberately cooking strong-smelling foods to retaliate). While his disregard for the shoe rule is disrespectful, your actions weren''t about maintaining household harmony-they were about getting back at him. Deliberately cooking pungent foods knowing it would upset him, especially as a form of payback, escalates conflict rather than resolving it. Mature cohabitation requires communication and compromise, not petty retaliation. You''re both at fault, but your intentional provocation makes you the bigger asshole here."]'::jsonb, '["46230516ce3150286ee21a928ad68293874d2dc846d7f0f81c94f5cf7ecf5982","bbdf9ffcb27edcb8bc6245f15554b623fac7cd1d7d17a646e0e5d0569e6a340f","abd9793bd5d0f0017c1ccf5f21357705992d827995fb3291ae73d4420973eafc","407ef9c23dc4d23340abe8e387a63554b9b399a9a8d1f5d4302e816d7db627c2","4cff93d03e5ac102a92b43e6e3f27462f307d16c87cc73615d7c2249db587e91"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"共同居住者是否能以付租金或在自己家应当舒适为由，拒绝既有协议或对室友作合理迁就","surfaceDifference":"A 是鞋规被破坏后用重味食物报复；B 是室友要求来访男友在场时改变居家穿着","directAnalogyRisk":"low"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-05', 5, 'primary', 20, 'qwfyu8', 'AITA for insisting the toilet lid should be put down before it is flushed?', 'I will start by mentioning that I am quite the neat/clean freak. When I think about a toilet flushing aggressively like a whirlpool, splashing poop particles and spreading them in the air, contaminating all the surfaces (especially tooth brushes), I am appalled.

I (29f) used to live with my friend (29f) who never had an issue with putting the lid down.

I recently moved in with my SO (31m) who has agreed to put the lid down, but isn’t consistent about it, which bothers me and I will remind him whenever he forgets. However, then he gets annoyed and says it’s not that important, which makes me feel dismissed/angry and wondering if maybe I’m the asshole for insisting people do this in my home.

I’m angered by the dismissive attitude because he agreed to put the lid down and told me I should remind him if he forgets, but then he gets upset with me for reminding him and tells me I shouldn’t be upset because it’s not a big deal. 

But I realize it is his home too, and even if I feel it is a small ask, it may be a bigger ask - given he has to change a regular habit to meet my request.

So…am I the asshole for insisting the lid be put down before flushing every time?', 'a587d0d48dc0c015b0be6179785bdd079fda9612284c8097e790a0edddd5bf05', '1ir168z', 'AITA for pointing out poor hygiene', '27 years old female dating 25 year old male for 1.5 years. A few months after we started dating i noticed that he would occasionally smell yeasty. It’s in his head/face are and i honestly have no idea where the source of origin is. Acne, ears, nose idk…Now i have the nose of a blood hound as well so many people probably wouldn’t notice 1/2 the smells that I do. I can smell a dirty diaper across a room. I also work in medical field so am very in tune with body smells. I hate yeast the smell is so nasty and gives me the ick. Every 3 months or so I notice it mention it he fixes it. Last night he was started to make out with me and I let him know he smelt yeasty again. He went to the bathroom and washed up and came back to bed and turned away from me. I’m pretty sure he was crying. It wasn’t that i didn’t want to be intimate with him, but it took everything in me not to start gagging and I feel like telling him was absolutely needed I just said, “you smell yeasty again”. (Which honestly I’ve smelt it the last 2 days but just got intimate with him then forgot the smell was much worse last night). 

Also he rewears clothes too much and they will often start to smell and I have to mention it to him so maybe that’s part of the issue some how idk. 

Outside of this he’s a great guy. Good husband material and pretty prefect in most other ways but the hygiene thing is going to be rough if it’s going to offend him and I feel like intimacy is severely compromised due to it. My perspective is if I stink someone had better tell me ASAP and maybe I’m too extra about smells like I put deodorant on 2x a day (morning and night) and he mentioned that was weird… so idk. ', 'e20d10d050102890c926a0afac6ac63c62c126e73803a5fd0121ddb8d60c6d6b', '["NAH But I''d really think about if this is the hill you want to die on.\n\nI personally prefer the lid down too, but millions of people leave it up and all of them are okay. So most of this is in your head (how close is your toothbrush to the toilet?).\n\nHow about investing in a lid that closes automatically? I had one of these in a previous apartment. No idea what they cost, but maybe look into it. Problem solved for everyone.","NTA - as someone who is not a neat freak or OCD, I recognize that particles are expelled into the air when you flush. Anyone that disagrees with me, I''m sorry I don''t have a before / after cleaning pic of the side of the sink''s counter that''s right next to my toilet","NTA. It''s actually much cleaner to keep the lid down. Like, science is a real thing. To me it''s not that important, but if it was to someone else and I lived with them I''d work hard to remember to close the lid. It just basic human decency.","NAH. I''m the same and I try to get everyone to comply. My husband was already shutting the lid before we met because he''d read a horrifying article about the germs. My mom visits a lot and I''ve tried to get her on board, but I''ve had to give up. She''s an amazing mom and she''s 70 and I''d be nothing without all the love she raised me with. Letting it go is an act of love. I just make sure nobody leaves their toothbrush out on the counter!\n\nIn your case, yeah, that auto shut lid is a great idea. You could also try a sign","NTA....I never put my toilet lid down until I had a roommate that educated me on the poo particle thing. Now I am aware of it, so I''m team lid down.\n\nIt sounds to me that your bf may need some education. May I suggest some Erin Brockovich sass be applied to the situation?\n\nFirstly, show bf poo particle, toilet flushing research - http://www.thisweekintomorrow.com/vol-2-no-32-1-skepticaltuesdays-aerosolized-poop-or-you-should-probably-close-the-lid/\n\nAnd then make the suggestion that you will flush with the lid open once after taking a dump with his toothbrush nearby. Let''s see what his reaction is then."]'::jsonb, '["39a206bcb98a6776d1bfe1cad44a793e2cd5d9364a5efc71dcffc1e72070ad36","9156175301ee60fd7ed959b4dcc094e0ce23398ab88240f6748c55b3e38d618d","6de764c24f395028d2fc00455289d905eb721f6922180aa68c8fa897e4ad2d35","1757f652bbdca9cc68047bd20f3668a33b6800cee31853e0b3a452f3b4e433ca","780a2c0bdb7d2d96f5847dbefe5b1cbe89de8cebdcbb0cbb9e253cee912ea12b"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYour request is based on a legitimate and scientifically-backed hygiene concern, as flushing with the lid up does aerosolize bacteria and waste particles. Your partner initially agreed to this boundary and then reneged, dismissing your valid feelings when you hold him to his word. His annoyance at being reminded, after he explicitly asked for reminders, makes him the asshole in this situation. It is a minor habit change for a significant gain in shared household cleanliness and respect for your peace of mind.","NTA\n\nThe request to put the toilet lid down before flushing is a reasonable hygiene concern, especially given the fear of aerosolized particles landing on surfaces like toothbrushes. Your partner agreed to the habit and even invited reminders, which makes reminding him appropriate rather than abusive. His annoyance stems more from inconvenience than from any real wrongdoing, and his dismissal of a legitimate health-related request is inconsiderate. Since you''re simply trying to keep your shared space clean and have asked for a simple habit change that he initially accepted, you''re not the asshole for insisting on it.","NTA\n\nYou have a valid concern about cleanliness and hygiene, and your partner agreed to put the lid down before flushing. It''s reasonable to remind him when he forgets, especially since he told you to do so. His inconsistent behavior and dismissive attitude are frustrating, and it''s not unfair to expect him to follow through on his agreement, especially in a shared living space where cleanliness affects both of you.","NTA\nYour request is reasonable and based on factual concerns about hygiene. Your boyfriend agreed to the rule and even encouraged you to remind him, so his annoyance when you follow through seems inconsistent and dismissive. While changing habits can be difficult, the issue isn''t the size of the ask-it''s his failure to honor his agreement and respect your feelings in a shared home. Communication and compromise are key, but you''re not wrong for insisting on a simple, agreed-upon measure that matters to you.","NTA\n\nYou''re not the asshole for wanting the toilet lid to be put down before flushing, especially since it''s a hygiene concern you''ve communicated clearly. Your partner agreed to this request but becomes annoyed when reminded, which is inconsistent and dismissive. It''s reasonable to expect follow-through on agreed-upon habits in a shared living space, particularly when it impacts your comfort and sense of cleanliness. His mixed signals-agreeing, then resenting reminders-create the conflict, not your request."]'::jsonb, '["b05d7d38d9fbe9eb5d3a4a5937e3c80bb17f0ccc2c233bc4faf003752d5871ab","5840d2d2afefba8e4c54c4405a442a09c601acbb2dc925d8671ccc89ba837b10","9bd4b491d93c894822eade40a1c3470d06e3532a13f389d85accf782d7e03ff9","cbf42561dfe41ff8d0385e783567c73e1befcf9a2afebd9e89c18eee222ad05f","b57679e091c953fc25e937c853766ed1ce899e2e3b38ea693d7a21aebc0439a0"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"伴侣之间如何提出卫生相关要求，并平衡健康或舒适、习惯改变、尊严与敏感沟通","surfaceDifference":"A 是冲水前放下马桶盖的共同习惯；B 是是否以及如何指出伴侣的体味问题","directAnalogyRisk":"medium"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-06', 6, 'primary', 70, 'rmeg2q', 'AITA for getting mad at my kid (17) for falling asleep on the couch with the oven on 450?', 'So the other day, my almost grown kid came into the kitchen where I was seated at the table working. They said they were going to make a pizza for lunch and that they were turning on the oven. I said ‘sure hon’ and went back to working. I half-heartedly heard the oven a little while later (as I’m partially deaf) and hollered out to them that it was preheated and ready. They didn’t come. 

My preschooler came in right then though, and distracted me for about 10 minutes and then I remembered the oven again and yelled for them again. This time with frustration thinking they’ve gone to their room downstairs, as they’re just nowhere around, no food is being cooked, but the stove is on full blast. 

Nope. They had gone to sleep on the couch. 

I got upset with them and yelled at them that, that wasn’t okay at all. They can’t just go to sleep with the oven on. Their only defense is that they were expecting me to tell them when the oven had beeped so they could come cook, and that I didn’t. 

I tried explaining  (angrily at this point but not yelling) that I most definitely did-they slept thru it, and that at 17, they should not be assuming anything or expecting people to do things for them like basic self-care and daily responsibilities. If they’re cooking…that is not the time to go take a nap on the couch as they wait for it to preheat.

I also explained how dangerous that is, and that if they were living on their own, they could’ve potentially burnt their home down, while they slept. 

Now they’re mad at me for being mad at them unnecessarily. Nothing happened, and I’m overreacting with anger about it.

I just want them to take some responsibility for themselves and their surroundings and to function at a degree they won’t hurt/kill their selves or others accidentally, when they move out some time next year. It’s not like I don’t teach them these things. 

So…. Are they just lazy or AITA??', '9231134dd9d481e638769bfc4d0c4bbce0ce021c3736bf1dc8ff69e3212bef3a', 'i41jt1', 'AITA I refused a boy grilled chicken when he did not say "please" and he hurt himself trying to take it with bare hands', 'I don''t know how much to squeeze into the title but hope that it is clear. This happened over the weekend when I helped with a party in my brother''s house among some of his friends and family.


I was in charge of grilling meat. When children came to me, some of them, would either ask for meat directly or just pass me their plate. When that happens, I would ask questions like "What''s the magic word that I missed?" Or "Should I hear a ''please''?" And most would remember to say "please" and "thank you" later.


So there came a little boy, maybe 8-10(not a good judge of age sorry), came to me and hold up his plate without a word. I reminded him, and he just glared at me and dropped the plate on top of the griller. Instead of passing him grilled chicken, I passed him a new plate told him "That''s not nice, you should say ''please''. No ''please'', no chicken. Let''s try that again." 


The boy stomped his feet and then without warning, reached out and grabbed a piece of grilled chicken from the pan. With a yelp and a jump the boy burst into tears clutching his left hand.


The boy''s parents came storming in when they heard him cry. As the mother brought the boy to rinse his hands and the father started to yell at me, demanding to know what happened. He was a big intimdating man, I got scared and was in a loss for words as he advanced before my brother jumped in between us.


I explained to them what happened and the father told me that the boy was not mine to educate, that I should just have given him the meat. The mother came back with the crying boy whose left hand were red in a few areas.


The parents demanded that I take responsibility over their son''s injury, but I told them that if their son was this unpredictable they should have him supervised at all times. The other children had no problem with my ways and they should be responsible for their son. The parents got angrier and my brother told me to leave and let him handle this.


I am not sure what happened later on because I went upstairs with SIL and later on my brother assured me that everything is alright, as if I am a kid myself. AITA in that situation? Hope he''s not in trouble but he wouldn''t tell me.', 'ffdc1aa4890704b1a7135840ceb0a365228789e940cfb26bfe1c7251d82c665e', '["NTA\n\nThe 17 years old should know how to take care of the food they''re preparing themselves. You could''ve gone somewhere, you could''ve been busy with something else, finally - you admitted yourself that you are partially deaf, so you might have not even heard that beep! You aren''t your kid''s alarm clock!","NTA. We basically kicked my husband''s mother out of our home when she left a broth cooking on the stove and left the house for several hours when we weren''t there.\n\nI Stan anyone who makes a big deal out of stove/oven safety. I''ve literally seen homes burn to the ground because someone left their oven on.","ESH. I get your point. You shouldn''t go to sleep with the oven on, but dude shit happens. There is no need to yell about things like that. My mother leaves the oven on all the time, no harm no foul, turn it off, move on.\n\nI get he is 17, he should be paying attention, but there is also no need for yelling over something that is honestly a relatively small problem. (And as someone pointed out, ovens are designed to be on for long periods without catching flame).","ESH. You definitely over reacted because even if the oven was on for hours the house wasn''t going to burn down especially if there was no food in it\n\nBut they should take responsibility for their actions and pay attention, the toddler could have possibly gotten the oven open and been burned","YTA but not actually an asshole. I do think you overreacted and this could have been more of a teaching moment instead of a fight, though. If you take the opportunity to apologize for the yelling and overreaction, it could do a lot to show your kid how to better handle situations in the future and smooth things over with better communication."]'::jsonb, '["87c090a4b7334f6f4c9b23ba98c24bd308b17f564956fe108ab0fcd2f7445555","f69e27d1c816d628fe632c60701d1f7297f22890ac988b5309634b76fb8b8eb0","47bd4b17b3dfb450d59767c5f3da826c6fc76dead8e44a5fb1f1394d292cab54","56dc31302c5a4037860ea72d3c20b567badb792fee584d4fc2fa3ad8a33ef299","8777404f861fae780a6e27b980ebc99cdd161b4db57521f5d3d2dbef3c865d4a"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYour reaction was justified given the serious safety risk. A 17-year-old is old enough to take responsibility for an appliance they turned on, and falling asleep while waiting for an oven to preheat is negligent. Your anger stems from concern for their safety and a desire to prepare them for living independently, which is a parent''s responsibility. While their initial expectation to be notified might have been reasonable, their complete failure to follow through and then shifting blame onto you is not.","NTA\n\nThe teen was responsible for the oven they turned on. Leaving a hot oven on while falling asleep is unsafe and shows a lack of basic responsibility, especially for someone living independently soon. Even if the parent''s reminder was a bit delayed, the teen should have checked the oven themselves rather than assuming they''d be notified. The parent''s frustration, while perhaps a bit loud, wasn''t unreasonable given the potential fire hazard.","NTA\n\nThe OP has a legitimate concern for their child''s safety and responsibility, especially when it comes to cooking and using appliances. The child''s decision to go to sleep while the oven was on was reckless and posed a significant risk of fire or other accidents. The OP''s frustration and anger, although possibly excessive, were driven by a desire to teach their child an important lesson about responsibility and self-care. The child''s expectation that someone else would monitor the oven and notify them when it was ready is unreasonable and underscores the OP''s point about their need to take responsibility for themselves. The OP''s concerns about their child''s ability to function independently when they move out are also valid, making their reaction not entirely unjustified.","NTA\nYour reaction was completely justified. A 17-year-old is nearly an adult and should understand basic safety, especially when operating a potentially dangerous appliance like an oven. Leaving a 450-degree oven unattended to take a nap is a serious fire hazard, regardless of whether they thought you would alert them. Your anger wasn''t about them falling asleep; it was about the dangerous lack of responsibility. Your kid needs to learn that safety is their own responsibility, not someone else''s, and this was a critical lesson. You''re not overreacting; you''re parenting to ensure they don''t harm themselves or others when they''re on their own.","NTA\n\nYou''re not the asshole for being upset in this situation. At 17, your child is nearing adulthood and should be expected to handle basic responsibilities like cooking safely. Leaving an oven on at 450 degrees F while falling asleep is a serious fire hazard and could have led to catastrophic consequences. Your frustration is valid, especially since you already alerted them that the oven was preheated. The expectation that you would monitor the oven and wake them defeats the purpose of them taking responsibility for their own actions. Teaching accountability and safety now is crucial, especially as they prepare to live independently. Your reaction, while angry, was rooted in legitimate concern for safety and responsibility, not overreaction."]'::jsonb, '["5e8797e4dd6a016c39b52105df8afaf76b28ad2eb53d0ea56938d7082c8defcf","dc5fa948827a238c6a3fb42a860b00570042be64d8b77d3d88c726249758e417","6d33e47fc4a5ed7f5afed826230fc8fc246e87bd91a193a538d7c8e65b1def98","fe5f84fa9178b02d1a14e84c886f1973d839a843060b7174758401012eb6d135","ff722b54066f8e1a027655f0da5dc43206d869dc45e02f59ac268d71aaa39441"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"年轻人发生危险或违规行为时，责任应如何在年轻人与监督成人之间分配，以及愤怒、惩戒或拒绝帮助何时过度","surfaceDifference":"A 是自己的十七岁孩子开着烤箱睡着；B 是他人年幼孩子无视指示抓热食并受伤","directAnalogyRisk":"low"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-07', 7, 'primary', 3, '110shnn', 'AITA for insisting that my friend--who is a big eater--pay for more food than everyone else?', 'Two of my friends (let''s call them Connor and Alice) and I are getting together tonight and having pizza for dinner. Alice eats more than anyone I know (she is an athlete with an insane metabolism and eats a lot in one sitting). Whenever we have shared pizza in the past, Alice ends up eating what is probably more than her fair share simply because she is so hungry. She also only ever wants pepperoni pizza, which I''m not a huge fan of. I prefer chicken and veggie pizzas, which no one else ever wants, so I often begrudgingly agree to eat pepperoni when I''d much rather be eating something else.

I asked Alice, who is ordering the pizzas, if Connor and I could split a large pizza so that I could get my preferred toppings, he could get his, and Alice could get a medium pepperoni all to herself. She responded by asking if we could instead split an extra large for all three of us. I don''t want to do this because:

* Alice would undoubtedly order pepperoni for half, which Connor and I don''t particularly like, meaning she''d get half the pizza all to herself. She is pretty picky and often does not want anything besides pepperoni, so Connor and I wouldn''t get nearly as much pizza for ourselves this way.

* This would also mean that Connor and I would have to compromise on the other half, when I really just want my darn chicken and veggie pizza and I know Connor won''t go for it (he is mostly vegetarian), meaning I''d have to once again give up my preferred toppings to appease the rest of the group.

I doubled down and was a bit insistent that we do it the way I suggested, explaining that I rarely got to order my preferred toppings and that this way everyone would get what they wanted. Alice seemed a bit put off that I suggested it but seemed to reluctantly agree. She is a bit short on cash right now, but the price difference is pretty minimal--it''s $20 for her to get a medium pepperoni for herself, whereas it would be around $15-17 for half of an extra large (that she would definitely end up eating half of herself), so it''s really only a difference of a few bucks.

AITA for suggesting we do it this way? I''m realizing that Alice may have been under the impression that we''d split the extra large three ways, but I don''t think she necessarily realizes that she often eats a lot more than everyone else and it would be more fair for her to pay for more pizza since she tends to eat bigger portions.', '2674f8940bc6425c242d9f2dd87ff435e5941a0772ee3a6c873630bd5b8bde40', 'zy1vr3', 'AITA for requesting my parents to turn on the heat?', 'I (26f) live and work in the US, but my parents live in Germany. I am here for the holidays and staying at my parent’s house (my childhood home). We have a good relationship, I visit every year for Christmas and usually one or two other times every year. 

Due to current affairs (war in Ukraine etc), electricity and especially gas prices (as in gas for heating the house) have gone up a lot - around 40%. My parents live alone and got used to very cold temperatures in the house in an effort to keep costs as low as possible. They only heat the living room if my brother, SIL and nephews (1m and 3m) visit. The rest of the house is not heated at all. 

When I came home, they turned on the heat in my room prior to my arrival so it was comfortable when I got here. But now, they turned off the main heating thingy, which means even if I turn on the radiator in my bedroom, the heat only comes on when they decide to turn it back on downstairs.

I am extremely cold all of the time, even with multiple sweaters and socks and with a blanket wrapped around my shoulders, etc., I am freezing. This makes it really hard for me to do anything or be productive because I just want to be huddled under a blanket all the time. I’ve told them this but they said to just get used to it. I feel that it is unfair that they cater to other visitors (my brother and his family), but not to me. 

AITA for complaining about this?', '946179146fc8ee5b268797c42344ca7323418ab2934996bebe7935188593b3d1', '["NTA... but, I''ve gotta say, you are lucky if this is what counts for a \"problem\" in your life.\n\nAlso, why didn''t you just order a small pizza with your favourite toppings? I don''t see the need to split if you all have different tastes / appetites.","NTA.\nI''m like her, bottomless pit of a stomach. I never want to share a pizza because I never want to be in the position of being still hungry and wanting to eat more than my share.\n\nOrder what you want, it''s ok to want to eat all of it.","NTA. She has a very specific and different diet than you and Connor, she should be aware of that and manage her own food. Maybe pizza isn''t the best thing to constantly get since it requires so many options and specific choices across you three.","NAH. I''m all for spending money wisely, but you''re not if you''re buying things you don''t want, or don''t benefit from.\n\nEach of your order a pizza. You each eat from the pizza you ordered and paid for. You have it for lunch and dinner tomorrow too.","ESH. You''re all complicating and scorekeeping way too much for what should be a simple pizza night. Research the deals for your local places and let everyone choose toppings for a medium, small, or personal sized pizza. Whatever you can find that is affordable and fair. Or go Aldi and get their $5-7 each take and bakes and bake them yourself."]'::jsonb, '["54d96bdea7b9163be042458e80db099b25fd6318468d02e0d593bd6eb72dc1db","26b38d210699a1fd35bedfeb7a389535bea25d9f309a2b7d766a210ebad46a09","f48e844df9da467e9081ce45ccee3b27524b1ca18b2f110826f394b1c1bdf743","973e9f5da7b70b235dd192ad2b2dea29660ff40fcc6a041d82167e76fc93a895","450eb9dbc06407f672a3f46ea5eeb6451115def3bd300d3cc238ffed2560cfbe"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYou proposed a fair solution that accommodates everyone''s preferences and appetites. Since Alice consistently eats a larger share of the shared pizza and insists on a topping only she prefers, it is reasonable for her to pay for her own dedicated pizza. This ensures you and Connor also get to enjoy your preferred toppings without having to compromise or end up with less food. Her financial constraint, while unfortunate, doesn''t outweigh the need for a fair arrangement, especially since the cost difference to her is minimal.","YTA\n\nThe request is primarily about getting the OP''s preferred toppings without considering the needs or constraints of the others-especially Alice, who''s both a big eater and short on cash. By insisting on a separate pizza for just the two of them, the OP is forcing Alice to either pay extra for a medium she''ll likely eat all of herself or accept a larger share of a pizza she won''t enjoy. This is a selfish compromise that dismisses the group''s collective decision and places an unfair burden on Alice. A more reasonable approach would be to find a middle ground (e.g., ordering a larger pizza with a mix of toppings or sharing a half-and-half) rather than insisting on a split that serves only the OP''s preferences. Hence, the OP is being the asshole.","NTA\n\nYou made a reasonable suggestion that would allow everyone to get their preferred toppings, and it''s not unfair to ask Alice to consider getting her own pizza given her large appetite and picky eating habits. The price difference is minimal, and it''s not like you''re asking her to break the bank. By doubling down on your suggestion, you were advocating for yourself and Connor to get the toppings you want, which is a fair request. Alice''s initial hesitation and reluctance to agree may be due to her not wanting to pay a bit more, but it''s not an unreasonable ask considering her eating habits.","NTA\nYou proposed a solution where everyone gets exactly what they want to eat, which is often the goal of group ordering. It''s not about her eating more; it''s about ensuring you don''t have to compromise on toppings and portion size. Alice wants pepperoni, you want chicken and veggie, and Connor wants vegetarian. Splitting pizzas this way is fair to everyone''s preferences, not just appetites. The cost difference is minimal, and it avoids you and Connor subsidizing a pizza you don''t even like. You were accommodating by suggesting she get her own medium rather than insisting she pay more for a shared pizza, which is a perfectly reasonable compromise.","NTA\n\nYou''re not being unreasonable for wanting to enjoy your preferred pizza toppings and for suggesting a fairer distribution of food and cost based on actual consumption. Alice eats significantly more and has very specific preferences, which already limits options for the group. Your suggestion allows everyone to get what they want without forcing compromises you and Connor don''t want. The cost difference is minimal, and it''s fair that someone who eats more pays a bit more-especially since she''d be consuming nearly half the food if it were split three ways anyway. You''re not attacking her or being hostile; you''re advocating for a practical solution. She may feel sensitive about money, but your request isn''t financially burdensome or malicious. You''re simply asking for fairness in food and choice, which is reasonable. NTA."]'::jsonb, '["93c76d8f53a77411bf68210263d787de52860c0930b0e510e510c3441538d314","fd0ef1c7b659d5b5ca4836396ec54c729d3d9f54011c8a9b88d935a94f74a280","d42b2ddf679d0fc42f9a26bc52b2088efc7da67847a7d093f676c50a8fe7de06","f766b70b39b9ea2a36f2e97724f2592ca91e05de4ed1e8cf4779b1e047c6245c","b6f1c1610b7f261391966317c57e3b536c0707945cc8acfe9b4003d495c144d9"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"共享资源成本与个体需求不同时，应按使用量、需求、平等还是待客规范来分配","surfaceDifference":"A 是食量与口味不同的朋友如何分摊披萨；B 是能源昂贵时更怕冷的成年访客能否要求父母开暖气","directAnalogyRisk":"low"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-08', 8, 'primary', 72, 'iwbjqd', 'AITA - Boyfriend chose weed over an anniversary gift.', 'Me (19F) and my boyfriend (21M) have been together a year on the 30th. It''s not long I know, but he lives in a little village and doesnt have a job. Hes 21. I live in the city and had a job but have been on furlough since march. I''m 19. I''ve spent the last 8 months creating a scrapbook and a little box of his favorite items and things that he''ll like and I know he hasn''t gotten me anything yet. Hasn''t even thought of it. He doesnt have any money but I told him he could just get me a card or make me something personal. I''m not materialistic at all but it would be nice to get something in return for a project that took 8 months to complete. His grandma gave him £10 this week when I was next to him and he told me hes get me a card and a little something, even if it was cheap. I was more than okay with that. Half an hour later he told me hed be back in ten minutes. He spent the £10 on weed. When I called him out on it he said that hed borrow some money from his grandma or mum to get me a card but that just makes me feel like shit because it''s not from him and it, and I, wasnt really his first choice. Weed was. I know technically the £10 wasnt from him either but everytime I mention going on universal credit or jobseekers he just shrugs it off. He is looking for a job, but it seems half assed.

TDR - AITA for expecting my boyfriend to get me a gift on our one year anniversary or is He TA for choosing weed instead?', '1f0131a34894ed584be84c2cbac7ef7304476736f5f48bbade51f2b18c364d31', '16ht7af', 'AITA for saying I would like a push present?', 'Okay so this happened a few months ago but it still bothers me and I would like some opinions 

Two months ago I (F27) gave birth to my first child with my partner (M31) 

Leading up to the birth I saw various tik tok videos about men buying their pregnant partners push presents after they''d just given birth. It ranged from expensive jewellery to the partners fave food that they hadn''t been able to eat since being pregnant. 

I showed it to my partner and said how sweet I thought it was, and jokingly asked what he would be getting me for a push present? 

He was really dismissive. Just said I wouldn''t be getting anything, the baby was the present. 

I''ll be honest, I was kinda hurt by his response. I''m not expecting diamonds or anything, but I feel like a bunch of flowers or something  small would be a really sweet symbol of gratitude for the pain I have gone through and still would go through to bring our child into the world. But he was having none of it, and made out like I was really spoiled for wanting a push present. 

He then bought it up with my mum the next time we saw her and they were both laughing at me basically again saying I was so spoiled to expect a push present. 

AITA for thinking it would have been a sweet thing to get a little gift from my partner to show his appreciation of what I would have been through being pregnant and giving birth?', 'e110b8ab9f920a379320195aa6343688e3fd49653634b2818f9c843d74e2b938', '["ESH\n\nYou spent 8 months of a 1 year relationship making a gift.... that''s odd. You want a present that is equal in thought in return because you''ve spent 2/3 of your relationship making one gift.\n\nHave you guys ever talked about where you see your relationship? You seem like you are trying to build a future with him, and honestly, he seems like he is just about being casual. If this story was told from another perspective you would come over as almost stalkerish. I think you need to take a step back.","NTA.\n\nYou seem really sweet, so I hate to have to tell you this, but you''re not in a relationship. You seem to think you are, but to be in a relationship, you actually need two people investing, not just one. It''s about time you realize that and find a nice guy have a real relationship with.","INFO - Is he aware that you''ve been working for 8 months for year 1 anniversary? If he is, he might be trying to get you to break things off because he might be worried that you''ll fly off the handle if he breaks up with you.\n\nTo me this sounds super obsessive and creepy. While this certainly might be youthful excitement, I''ve seen enough to be seriously worried the relationship will drag me, my friends and my family into a drawn out shit show.","NTA AT ALL. He does not sound ready to be in a serious relationship. This is a warning that he will not prioritize you as highly as you prioritize him in the future. He sounds like a thoughtless AH who doesn''t consider your feelings as important as GBP 10 of weed.","NTA. This not only shows his emotional immaturity, but his lack of priorities and the inability to have a successful relationship. This is not on you, and someone who cares more about some trash weed of your relationship on your birthday is someone who is not a good boyfriend, and will never be until he grows up. OP, I would get out of this relationship immediately. If he can''t see that what he did was wrong, and just defends his shitty decisions, dump him. He doesn''t deserve you"]'::jsonb, '["e42086416d45a940c0225098163c5767fe2ad2876844e61f603231726032ab73","56a2f68a81a406aae6bc4a0da0d9e8f934b9d45a5924cd1d25ef549ad953bbbf","4b0ccbad1ebfeec5bc3a83d3851ccca70871401b871603bb6840833a9a4001a1","7674c43530b700f2b7366d2ae3bf978f3f105f39828975eb3df006d9231ebe99","301109ee568b57bcf047080aba4efa317fd1a3723b369fc94862a6b2d7c4d081"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYour boyfriend had a clear choice between acknowledging your one-year anniversary with a simple, thoughtful gesture and buying weed. He chose the weed, knowing it would hurt you. While the money itself was a gift, his decision to spend it on himself after explicitly saying he would use it for you shows a lack of consideration and respect for your feelings and the effort you put into your gift. His offer to borrow money for a card afterward only reinforces that you and the anniversary are not a priority.","NTA\n\nYou''re not demanding anything extravagant-just a simple, personal card or a small token, especially after putting in eight months of effort yourself. Your boyfriend''s choice to spend the only money he had (even if it was his grandma''s) on weed, rather than give you even a modest, heartfelt gesture, shows a lack of respect and prioritization of your relationship. While his financial situation is strained, a handmade card costs nothing and would have demonstrated care. Expecting a small, thoughtful gift in return for your effort is reasonable, and his actions are inconsiderate.","YTA\n\nHe explicitly told you he would get you a card and a little something with the GBP 10 his grandma gave him, and then he spent it on weed instead, which is a clear indication of his priorities. However, considering his financial situation and your expectations, it''s also important to recognize that you''ve been working on a project for 8 months, which might have built up high expectations. Your boyfriend''s actions, although thoughtless, might also be a result of his own financial struggles and feeling pressured to celebrate the anniversary in a way that feels meaningful to you. Given the circumstances, it seems like there''s a lack of understanding and communication about each other''s priorities and financial constraints, which might make the situation more nuanced than a simple right or wrong.","NTA\n\nYou communicated your expectations clearly and were very reasonable. You didn''t ask for an expensive gift; you just wanted a small gesture of appreciation, and even that was too much for him. The issue isn''t really about the money or the GBP 10-it''s about his priorities and lack of effort. Choosing weed over a promise to you, especially when you''ve invested so much time and thought into his gift, shows a lack of respect and consideration. His excuse to borrow money from his grandma only makes it worse, as it shifts the responsibility and turns your gift into an afterthought rather than a sincere gesture. You''re right to feel hurt by his choices.","YTA\n\nYou''re expecting a gift from someone who has no income and is living in a difficult situation, and you''re upset that he used the only money he received to buy something for himself instead of a gift for you. While it''s understandable to want recognition for your efforts, spending 8 months on a gift doesn''t obligate him to reciprocate, especially when he''s openly stated he can''t afford anything and you knew that going in. You even acknowledged a simple card or handmade gift would suffice, yet you''re still hurt that he didn''t follow through-even though he planned to borrow money to make it happen. Choosing weed doesn''t make him a bad person; it may indicate coping mechanisms in a stagnant environment. You''re placing emotional pressure on someone with limited means, and your disappointment seems disproportionate to the circumstances. Your actions aren''t malicious, but your expectations in this context are unreasonable."]'::jsonb, '["48f4c8d96238e96a6856b015a52fe1ff750a414d4cdd63c87ca309eaad9b8e03","9b8300b3dbe7200d28bcaf6b40422b21a45077b2f1948f706cdd41aece6374e3","549e50d2b6d9352ee4d9d659308cf70cab68b8239a71260d51f3721c7059e74a","87b617d491f10dc19df3cb639a95cba10be19760b71b25938edba03a12077701","e0a72ec39b622930819ac1781ce4b343b627cd9536035d5688b8c4c1bd4664ff"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"浪漫伴侣是否负有用小型象征性礼物表达认可和感激的义务，以及期待礼物何时会被视为理所当然","surfaceDifference":"A 是周年纪念时把钱花在大麻而非卡片；B 是生育后提出希望收到小型 push present","directAnalogyRisk":"medium"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-09', 9, 'primary', 50, 'w0gtvg', 'AITA for stopping attending my husband''s softball games?', 'My (26F) husband (31M) loves softball. He generally loves sports and is competitive, but his friend group created a softball league that has grown fairly large- so he plays with them to have fun/ exercise. (The league is just for fun and only recreational). I had no problem with this until I  realized just how much he loves to play. When we got married, we planned for a 10 day honeymoon, and my husband begged me to cut it in half the day before our wedding because he found out the league was starting the season during our honeymoon And wanted to support his team. I was incredibly hurt that he wanted to cut our honeymoon in half to play softball with his friends and felt that he was making them and the game a priority over me. I told him this and he still begged me throughout the week of our honeymoon to let him go. Eventually I gave up and we cut the honeymoon short but he felt guilty afterwards and opted not to go play. We spent the second half of our honeymoon at home.

As I got to know the friends my husband plays softball with better, I started to dislike them. One friend on the team encouraged my husband to postpone his elbow surgery in order to play softball. My husband listened to him even though I was not comfortable and felt that playing could worsen his elbow injury. He refused to go to the doctor to at least validate that playing would not negatively affect his elbow. I also found out that the manager of the team gossiped heavily about us while we were dating which caused some awful lies to be spread about my husband.  I really didn''t want to be around them anymore since it was obvious they didn''t really care about my husband or were his real friends.

Another team member''s wife was my one friend to keep my company while I went with my husband to the softball games but she and her husband ended up divorcing (long story), so I had no one there to keep me company anymore. 

For all these reasons, (not feeling like a priority, not liking his friends on the team, and feeling lonely at games) I told my husband I did not feel comfortable going with him to the games anymore. He became very upset and told me he feels like I''m making him choose between Softball and nothing. I assured him that''s not true and that he can play whenever he wants and I don''t feel it''s my place to "take away" something that he loves so much. It''s just that I don''t feel comfy being there anymore. I just feel sad. He still doesn''t like that I don''t want to be there and feels that I am not supporting him.

AITA for not supporting my husband by going to his softball games?', 'fa5f1520124bfe38e361def187abe7a11de9198365aaa22ee6a8dfabb10c4266', 'uvz5uf', 'AITA for not standing up for my man?', 'My fiancée (25 M) and I (24 F) went out to eat at a steak house. On the steak presentation glass there was 3 steaks with numbers on them one had the number 17 on it. He asked the host how could he get that specific $17 steak. She said to ask your server to reserve it for you. Keep in mind he said $17. Then another host sat us down he again repeated he wanted that $17 steak. She wrote it on the ticket and gave it to him to give it to our server (we was assumed she was our server not another host). We sat down ordered our food gave our server his ticket and went from there to wait for our food. I looked at the charge on the tablet on our table and low and behold it was a $30 steak. I told him they probably just didn’t put the right price on it but it was charged for a 17oz steak. I then mentioned maybe that wasn’t the price and it was the oz. the host(s) never corrected him when he said $17. Anyways he stopped our server told her the issue and a manager was called. He explained the situation and she said they dont have prices on the steaks that it was oz. Well that’s when it went downhill. He started complaining saying it’s not his fault he was told something else and never corrected when he said it twice to two different females. She apologized and sad it was a misunderstanding. He didn’t care he was told it was $17. I then told him after she left to stop being a dick and being embarrassing. Another manager then came he explained it raising his voice again and cussing. The manager then apologized that he doesn’t know why they wouldn’t correct him. My fiancée didn’t care he was standing his ground. The manager told him he couldn’t lower a $30 steak for $17. After going back and forth and ALOT of stares from other tables the manager said he will give him a 12oz for $17 instead. My fiancé apologized and thanked him and said “I’m not trying to be a dick and y’all are awesome Im not mad”  Yea ok… after the manager left I again said this is fucking embarrassing for a steak who cares it’s wrong communication get a different steak no one would’ve made you pay $30  if you just gotten a different one instead. But it’s the principle for him. We argued all night he wouldn’t live it down that I didn’t stick by his side even if he was wrong or embarrassing that I should’ve agreed with him because we’re in this forever. AITA?', '008600bc3c13da22d49ed8631b0f61a376cdebccfd6b1cb1ad2920b02fe0307f', '["INFO Just to clarify in case I misread your post: Your husband cut your honeymoon short for a fucking softball game?\n\nI know I tend to overreact, but the word ''annulment'' would have fleetingly entered my mind. If it''s an option marriage counselling stat. NTA.","NTA\n\nHe married you, but he''s having an affair with a ball game. He is incredibly selfish even though he probably thinks he''s doing it \"for the team\" except that he doesn''t realize/remember/care that he has a more important team in you.\n\nFind your own hobby. If he can throw his whole life into softball, you have every right to throw your whole life into something that interests and satiates you.","NTA - \"we planned for a 10 day honeymoon, and my husband begged me to cut it in half the day before our wedding because he found out the league was starting\" - this man is insane. And he postponed surgery? This is one of those guys with no other content in his life besides sports. And hey I like sports! Me and my wife just moved to a new city and one of the draws of this city was a sports club I wanted to join. I go to practice or a match twice a week and it''s where most of my social life mostly comes from. I wouldn''t cancel my honeymoon for it and guess what? I got an injury I played once with then when I realized it was serious I got medical attention and can''t play for a while. This guy isn''t into softball; he''s not into life and he thinks putting it all into softball makes it ok","NTA\n\nGet your own thing going. Yoga, hikes, book club, knitting.\n\nBonus if you book a place outside of home to go do it. But also marriage counseling because it seems there''s a gap forming between you guys, don''t let his bad company fill his head with nonsense.","Nta.\nSoftball games are long and boring to watch if you aren''t interested in softball. You''ve lost your friend to go to games with and sitting through a game without anyone to talk to and pass the time with sounds like torture.\nYour husband is ta and is putting his games over everything else in life.. even your honeymoon (idk how you didn''t leave him after that tbh)."]'::jsonb, '["ddcdcd68bc6caf5ea7586184e2ce094122e0af8b312e0bae15dba6a7b13a4c5f","0bdd292592e6b3add77b8fb3609cbad13c51a54735189884d68ac5b88d8eae9f","5ee54a77291671adbdfdffdff54c30c1cbc5a5a7641f9394ecbe8d1a9ddbd31a","9922aa02da75c66497f0755574e9846259dd6e73207f4f2bdb8443fa34c22094","cfd7e50efc1003f42c37b0a616b0b099ea0a630e2214cc35e89a43b0a1db9ff7"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYour husband has repeatedly prioritized his softball league and friends over your relationship, from cutting your honeymoon short to ignoring your valid concerns about his health. You are not obligated to spend your free time in an environment where you feel uncomfortable, unsupported, and lonely. Supporting his hobby does not require your physical presence at every game, especially when his teammates have shown themselves to be poor influences who disrespect your relationship. His attempt to frame your reasonable boundary as you making him \"choose\" is unfair.","NTA\n\nYour husband''s hobby isn''t something you''re obligated to attend, especially after he already sacrificed part of your honeymoon for it. The league''s start date clashed with a major life event, and he initially chose the game over your shared plans, which was reasonable to feel hurt about. As you got to know his teammates, you discovered genuine issues: they encouraged him to delay needed elbow surgery, and the manager spread false gossip. Those are legitimate red flags that make attending uncomfortable and potentially harmful to his health. You communicated your feelings, gave him space to play without you, and didn''t force him to quit the sport. His frustration comes from expecting you to support him in a setting that now feels toxic, not from any wrongdoing on your part. Therefore, you''re not the asshole for choosing not to go to his softball games.","NTA\n\nYour husband''s priorities and actions, such as wanting to cut the honeymoon short for softball and potentially worsening his elbow injury to play, have shown that his love for softball sometimes outweighs his consideration for you and your feelings. Given the negative experiences you''ve had with his friends on the team, including gossip and poor advice, it''s reasonable that you wouldn''t feel comfortable attending the games. You''ve also made it clear that you''re not trying to stop him from playing, but rather, you''re setting a boundary for yourself regarding your attendance. This doesn''t mean you''re not supporting him; it means you''re prioritizing your own emotional well-being, which is important in any relationship.","NTA\n\nYour husband has consistently prioritized this \"recreational\" softball league over significant moments and even his own health. The honeymoon incident alone was a massive red flag - demanding to cut your honeymoon short for a casual sports game shows a serious lack of priority-setting. His friends on the team have demonstrated they don''t have his best interests at heart, with one actively encouraging him to postpone necessary medical treatment. You''re not asking him to stop playing; you''re simply setting a reasonable boundary to protect your own emotional well-being by choosing not to watch him with people who don''t respect him (or your marriage) and make you feel lonely. True support isn''t about blind attendance - it''s about caring for someone''s overall wellbeing, which includes their marriage and health. You''re not making him choose \"softball or nothing\" - you''re choosing yourself when he consistently fails to do so.","NTA\n\nYou''re not the asshole for prioritizing your emotional well-being and setting boundaries. Your husband repeatedly placed his softball games above your shared experiences, including asking to cut your honeymoon short-something deeply meaningful and once-in-a-lifetime. His friends have shown poor judgment, encouraging him to delay medical care and spreading harmful gossip, which justifies your discomfort around them. You''re not refusing to support him; you''ve made it clear he can continue playing, but you''re no longer attending due to valid personal reasons. Support doesn''t require enduring emotional distress or toxic environments. Your feelings are valid, and your choice to step back is reasonable."]'::jsonb, '["cabad9144c0960292fec72ad77c79a714f5a8dc61a0bcff97d2f73ce7850d9f7","f6cc3b5a82582fde5ccf58865074e138146945e997155fa15299e637410fd3c7","c2193e7f3625cf613f40b61ec92029c4655291aabfb5044c6c6015f7930bafcf","f8e7eb6e85d7da6335cfa04b0042c3d1e291055712cc2aa5a0357962f02f9075","f3c3d51d876229b595cb9825b792c18465aec2a21f98e27ae6ed0ed80e759a56"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"支持伴侣是否意味着必须出席或公开维护，即使伴侣的优先级或现场行为不合理","surfaceDifference":"A 是不再参加把蜜月和手术都让位给垒球的丈夫之比赛；B 是未在餐厅公开维护因价格误解而辱骂员工的未婚夫","directAnalogyRisk":"low"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-10', 10, 'primary', 65, 'f5clwc', 'AITA for not wanting to go to our high school reunion with my wife to fulfil her revenge fantasy?', 'My wife and I (both 37, female) have known each other since high school. We grew up in a very conservative part of the US so we were both extremely repressed and didn’t get together until well into our adulthood. We both left our hometown as soon as we were able to (me at 18 when I headed to college, she after college) and neither of us have really visited since. From what we know though—thanks to the power of the internet and social media—the area we grew up in is still incredibly conservative.

My wife recently got a Facebook message from one of our old classmates about a 20th high school reunion that is being organized in our hometown this summer. Looking at the event page it seems a lot of people from high school are going. All of our social media profiles are private so they likely have no idea we’re now together.

My wife immediately jumped to accept the invitation, and assumed I would be ecstatic as well to “surprise everyone about our marriage”. I told her I didn’t really wanna go because: 1) I actually had a pretty shitty time in high school and so I’m not particularly inclined to see old classmates I haven’t talked to in 20 years; 2) she may not even get the reaction she thinks she’ll get; 3) we live far away now and it would be an unplanned travel expense. Not that we’re extremely poor, but I’d rather we spend our money on an actual vacation instead of bumming around in our hometown for a week with the kids.

I told her all this, and also said that if *she* wishes to go on her own, she is more than welcome to. But she insisted that it wouldn’t be the same unless we’re both there. One thing that may be relevant here is that my wife hung out with the popular crowd in high school, and so she evidently sees going to a high school reunion arm-in-arm with a woman as some revenge fantasy (like, “oh, the prom queen is now a lesbian” or whatever). She’s not normally this high school obsessed but she has mentioned several times she wants to surprise our former classmates with our relationship and financial success. I said the purposeful showiness of this seemed a bit immature and we should just focus on the good life we have now.

Growing up gay in the 90s in a conservative town was obviously... not great, to put it mildly, and I understand that we each have our own way of coping with the homophobia we experienced. This just seems like a bit of a step back in my opinion. But at the same time, going to the reunion clearly means a lot to my wife, whereas I’m more apathetic. AITA?', '29e93aa8f291765ffa019cff30f1068ec1d49bfb678d02ce2fd41b4d9d6de068', '153wrm8', 'AITA :: Future SIL bachelorette trip is ridiculous', 'Tell me AITA?

Future SIL of my youngest brother is throwing not a bachelorette party, but a four-day vacation out of the state. There are 19 women going (she''s 32, second marriage). 19? Who knows 19 let alone wants to travel with that many women? She''s Texan. I''m Californian. Am I missing something about a Southern bachelorette party? I am her only future sister in law and we''re on a groupchat constantly going off planning every meal, every outfit. I have kids, a career, these Gen X''s do not. I can afford it, no issue, but I don''t want to travel even for half the time with them. They polled everyone a budget and now it''s a big to-do to pay up your portion... not yet said how much, but must be $1K. I made a comment of count me out until month before (it''s planned for spring break in March) because of work/kids and I will try to make an appearance and support! The pounced on me that "we want as many people there to celebrate this wedding etc etc" AITA for bailing? Do I go? Is this starting off on the wrong foot? I have to hang with her the rest of my life? Will she always fault me???? Also, I''m not a bridesmaid. I''m in the "wedding party" what is happening in Texas????', '8d3c5031e1ead096bc23861138dc3f63b7801a8026452fbe43ef88df8119e3b4', '["NAH. How long ago is recently? If it''s just been a couple of days then she hasn''t really had time to think about it that much. It sounds like it could be an impulsive decision. Once you start figuring out transportation, lodging, what to do with the kids, and whatever other expenses there might be, she may rethink whether or not it''s worth it. Personally, I''m on your side. Anyone worth talking to from high school, I still talk to. As for everyone else, I''m not about to go out of my way to see people I didn''t even like then.","NAH - I went to my 20th high school reunion, and it was a huge drag. I wasn''t the most popular kid in High School, and I''m pretty successful now.\n\nBut it wasn''t me telling my success story to a bunch of wide eyed former classmates, it was pretty much a straight return to that shitty high school feeling. No one cares that you are now successful, they mostly just reminisce about old times. I immediately felt excluded. My wife (who was in my high school class) hung out with a bunch of people she used to know, and had a good time, while I mostly sat my myself and wondered why I freaking came to the stupid thing.\n\nMy feeling is that if you go, your wife will have a good time, and you will not.","NAH, but your wife is mistaken about what will happen. People will not magically change their minds, if they''re so inclined. Some will be homophobic, and the rest will say, \"Huh, ok,\" and move on with their lives.\n\nIf she doesn''t want to actually connect with people, it''s honestly not worth going. If there ARE people she/you would like to reconnect with, maybe set up a small separate meetup for those people.","NAH. No one really pays that much attention to anyone else at a high school reunion, so whatever impact she''s hoping to make will undoubtedly fall flat. She could accomplish the same thing by posting about your relationship to FaceBook, so this seems like it would be a definite waste of money.","NTA you both had different experiences in high school and maybe you need to let her know. I had a similar experience to what you had I was bullied, called fgg0t along with other derogatory nick names, threatened and humiliated. I''ve never attended any of my reunions because I don''t care what those people think and don''t care what''s happened/happening to them. So if you had any experiences like what I had, no your definitely NTA"]'::jsonb, '["a8871e8a0e6bdacdc1dfdabd65dbe53497ae160bb60269db57ab4b6188833f8a","24ae5a315025700593cabd7a9d72a61236759dbfc5bc8e96b828d6e1e1a47289","5813d7f78932444611c110658ce6610b955556a60445f547834e5f7f8e5d03f4","8cc12e83ff7a4dc59beb94508d5a418c8feb36f3c05e812a9bae6214eaf0faab","e52ccc7a1a648c0229a56e1ab182fa26fdacc4519d11f6ebf262e039045c6e23"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYour reasons for not wanting to attend are completely valid: you had a bad high school experience, the potential reaction is uncertain, and the trip is a significant expense. Your wife''s desire to go seems rooted in a need for validation or revenge, which, while understandable given your shared past, is not a healthy or mature reason to pressure you into an event you''re uncomfortable with. You''ve offered a fair compromise by being supportive of her going alone, which she has refused. Her insistence on you both attending for the sake of a \"surprise\" makes her the asshole in this situation.","NTA\n\nThe husband''s reasons-unpleasant high-school memories, the financial cost, and a lack of personal interest-are reasonable, especially given they have kids and could use vacation time elsewhere. He''s not forcing his wife to stay home; he even offers that she can attend alone. While his wife''s desire to \"surprise\" old classmates is understandable for personal affirmation, it doesn''t obligate him to sacrifice his own comfort and financial priorities. Thus, he''s not the asshole for declining to go.","NTA\n\nYour reasoning for not wanting to attend the high school reunion is valid, considering your past experiences and current priorities. You''ve also given your wife the option to attend alone, showing that you''re not trying to dictate her actions. Your concern about the potential reaction from former classmates and the financial aspect of the trip are also legitimate. It''s understandable that you might view your wife''s desire to attend as a form of revenge or seeking validation, and it''s reasonable to express your feelings about it. Your suggestion to focus on your current life and well-being rather than seeking to prove a point to people from your past seems like a mature approach.","NTA\nYour feelings are valid. You''re not obligated to participate in an event that brings up negative memories and costs money you''d rather spend elsewhere, all for a \"revenge fantasy\" that may not even play out as your wife imagines. You''ve been reasonable in offering to support her if she wants to go alone, and you''re free to prioritize your current happiness over re-living a past you''ve both thankfully moved on from. It''s okay to want to leave the past in the past, and your wife''s desire for a dramatic reveal shouldn''t override your comfort and financial priorities.","NTA\n\nYou''re not the asshole for not wanting to attend a high school reunion that holds negative memories for you and doesn''t align with your values or interests. While your wife may find emotional significance in making a statement about her identity and success-especially given the conservative environment you both grew up in-you have every right to set boundaries around events that feel draining, costly, or regressive. Your concerns about the trip being more about a \"revenge fantasy\" than genuine connection or celebration are valid. You''ve offered a reasonable compromise by supporting her decision to go alone if she chooses, which shows respect for her feelings. Your desire to focus on your present happiness and avoid revisiting a painful past is understandable and mature, not selfish. You''re not obligated to participate in something that feels performative or emotionally taxing just because it''s meaningful to someone else."]'::jsonb, '["9ac4833cfe43ef4ef1be1eacfff1ee7a8054eaa65ca154445aa7eba6e77a40cb","b085abd65545718758968e27b09722c91beb50107065d7fa0a09fb4a190ff250","5ea6a89c084cc605341486d950cb7b6573b7ebe4df2f1e506707ae00962bbe5f","16902ce3ed30eff4704d6d7e0f8bdc5cbb517ce86e75446d6983917f29900ced","886029b1630c76a578150c9b7222cb2e20b90a218e0eab2e07706c866f12ef69"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"为了支持亲密关系，个人是否必须承担旅行、时间和金钱成本参加自己不愿参加的社交仪式","surfaceDifference":"A 是配偶希望共同参加高中同学会以完成复仇式展示；B 是是否参加未来弟媳的四天外州单身派对","directAnalogyRisk":"low"}'::jsonb, true),
('hammer-pair-11', 11, 'reserve', 74, '1cufm4w', 'AITA for telling my wife''s family they can''t cook in our Airbnb', 'My wife and I have 2 kids (6 and 3) and my wife has 2 siblings. One brother (living in Cali and soon to be married later this summer), one sister (married with 3 kids all under 10) and both her parents (still married).We are pretty close with my wife''s family, spend a lot of time with them and all the kid cousins get along super well. I have a very good relationship with everyone.

The brother is getting married later this summer in Southern California. The rest of the family, including us, live in the Midwest. We''ve planned a big 7 night family vacation down to SoCal for the wedding.

I proposed that our family (4 of us) + my sister in laws family (2 adults, 3 kids) and my wife''s parents (2 adults) rent a house for the 7 nights we will be in SoCal. My reasons were:

-More space for everybody instead of living out of a suitcase in a hotel for 7 nights  
-Have a kitchen available to cook and eat together instead of eating out for every single meal -My son has a severe anaphylactic allergy to nuts so eating out can often be stressful for us as a family.

Initially everyone was onboard. I estimated that renting a large enough house (via AirBnB) would be approx $300/night per family unit (So $900/night split 3 ways). There are cheaper ones but they would be too far from the wedding venue.

My sister-in-law and my parent-in-law learned that hotel rooms are available for approx $150/night. They''ve now bailed on the house idea and have reserved hotel rooms. It''s their money and I understand renting a house is a lot of money to pay above the hotel rates. I have no issue with them changing their mind.

My wife and I still want to stay in a house, mainly for access to a full kitchen, so we booked an Airbnb. The smaller house will cost us about $350/night. So we''ll be spending about $350 more than planned ($50 x 7 nights). We''ve booked the AirBnb and the free cancellation period has now expired. We can''t cancel without losing money.

When my wife''s family learned that we booked an AirBnb they said, "Oh great! We can come to your house to cook food". I said , "No. If you wanted to cook then you should have reserved a house with us, not make us pay the extra for accommodations with a kitchen and then take advantage of that. If you want to use the kitchen cover the extra $50/night we have to pay and then you can use it.".

They say I''m being unreasonable and greedy. AITA?

The dollar figures are not the real numbers but they get the story across.', '61c0881dbfb03ad0e3a1226cae70f3cd0192629596999ca27a3f59a34ef4def7', '1hiqrg1', 'AITA for stopping to share photos of my kids with my in-laws and not inviting them over anymore?', 'I (F, 36) have been married to my husband for many years, and we have three well-behaved kids (F,F,F) aged 3-8, who are the only grandchildren in the family. My in-laws live about an hour away from us. My MIL is a stay-at-home wife, and my FIL works on a corn farm with a flexible schedule, while both me and my husband have full-time jobs.

Since our first child was born, my in-laws have been very hands-off. They used to visit once every 3 months when we lived closer, and now they only come for the kids'' birthday parties twice a year. I''ve made efforts to include them in our lives. I''ve invited them to dinners, family outings, trips to the zoo, and kids'' games. Their responses are always excuses like "grandpa doesn''t want to go" or "we''re busy," despite MIL being a stay-at-home wife, not being engaged in any volunteering or care for anyone else, and knowing how to drive a car very well. When the kids want to have sleepover at her house, she says she wouldn''t have anything to feed them (they eat milk and cereal for breakfast)...

Despite the fact that she refuses to engage with the kids, MIL gets extremely jealous when we visit my family and stay with them for a month every summer. To the point that this past summer, she agreed to watch our dog for us and then backed out three days before our departure saying that she planned to go on a few trips (no trips took place FYI).

Since our kids were born, I''ve tried sharing our kids'' photos via Skype and text messages, but they (MIL) never respond, comment, or even say thank you. In contrast, my family, who lives on another continent, always engages with the photos I share.

The last straw was this Thanksgiving. They and my BIL agreed to join us for dinner but canceled two days before, saying they were invited to my husband''s aunt''s place instead. My BIL was invited there too, but we weren''t "because we have too many kids." And my MIL/FIL didn''t think that was insulting...

To add insult to injury, every Christmas for the last many years, my MIL gives me two old-lady-like sweaters, while her other two daughters receive presents they actually like and would use. It feels like she doesn''t even try to understand me or my tastes.

So, as a new year''s resolution, I''ve decided to stop sharing photos with them and inviting them over for anything or going to their house more than I have to. I''m tired of them living so close yet seeming to care so little about me or our family.

AITA for cutting off the photos and stopping the invitations?', 'e8b77bf56d2c05a3fb0da26383b9293b7c9adcdd6d74a4f8c126c003873052bf', '["NTA.\n\nRead the rules on the airbnb. There is a good chance than them even coming over will give you a nice fine/fee.\n\nWe always have issues finding rentals that allow neice/nephew/mil to come over for a game night/pizza night when visiting family.\n\nIt would fall under rules about \"parties\" or extra guests.","NTA. It''s not even about the cooking, it''s about privacy and personal space. You can''t just invite yourself to someone''s house/hotel room. It''s one thing to share a big space with your extended family, it''s a completely different thing to cram 10 people into a small place just because granpa wants to cook himself some eggs\n\nIf anything, I wouldn''t have put a price tag on it, I''d just say the place was too small to have people over","YTA for the unfairness of the initial split. You should have been splitting by rooms, not \"family units.\" Who would a 5-person family be paying the same as your wife''s parents, who are only two people? Or course they''d rather get a hotel room than subsidize your housing.","NAH Let them buy all ingredients to cook and count it even. Them feeding your family of 2 adults and 2 children in exchange for use of the kitchen. Done.\n\nIt''s petty and weird to deny them access without paying you. They didn''t convince you to pick a house, they were totally fine going without a kitchen. Enjoy some great family meals and make some memories.","Nta, please also keep in mind most hosts in SoCal limit the number of guests that can be in the unit and often they have a device that measures how many cell phones are present in the unit. If an extra fix or six cell phones show up the host gets an alert and you get kicked out without refund."]'::jsonb, '["4fbc30d7864abff488707d5f2d6272bdfbaf9d6021235e0c15023da715126c78","8c737999ea360264761f57df24dde4f7d36929c4a57a09ad47201c43c66a7a64","f56c3e5e0d4be477ec2055b1bafd073a44d5904525e985cff1c1b75948128554","79b672dadd3772259704a063734684d5195320c688d00a2bb3605b9f6cc5eb93","f6305aa9861ea88fa9faa5ae0f0f841fa1edd6c365c31bdb377ee6a29cdd4ce5"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYou made a reasonable proposal for a shared house that would benefit everyone, particularly given your son''s severe allergy. They chose the cheaper hotel option, which is their right, but that choice came with the consequence of not having a kitchen. It is not greedy to expect them to contribute to the cost of the amenity they now want to use, especially since you are bearing the entire financial burden for it. They are the ones trying to get the benefit without sharing the cost.","NTA\n\nThe other families chose to stay in hotels and explicitly opted out of the house rental. You paid extra to secure a place with a kitchen for legitimate reasons (allergy concerns, convenience). It''s reasonable to expect them to either stay in their hotels or contribute to the cost if they want to use the kitchen you''re paying for. Refusing free use of the kitchen isn''t greedy-it''s just asking them to cover the expense they''re benefiting from.","NTA\n\nYou initially proposed a house rental that would benefit everyone, including your son''s food allergy, and everyone agreed. However, when others backed out due to cost, you and your wife still chose to rent a house for your family''s needs. Now, they want to use the kitchen without contributing to the extra cost. Your request for them to cover the extra $50/night is reasonable, considering you''re shouldering the additional expense for a house with a kitchen. It''s not unfair to expect them to pay their share if they want to use the facilities.","NTA\n\nYou and your wife made a financial and logistical decision to rent a house for your family''s specific needs, most notably your son''s severe nut allergy which makes cooking a necessity, not a luxury. You presented the option to the group, and they consciously chose to opt out in favor of cheaper hotels. They cannot then expect to reap the primary benefit of your more expensive accommodation (the kitchen) without contributing to the cost that made that benefit possible for you. You essentially offered them a share in a timeshare, they declined, and now they want to use the facilities for free. Your response was a direct and fair consequence of their initial decision. It''s not greedy to expect people to pay for the amenities they want to use; it''s simply setting a reasonable boundary.","NTA\n\nYou''re not the asshole because you made a reasonable proposal for shared accommodations that benefited everyone, especially given your child''s severe allergy. When your wife''s family chose to prioritize saving money by booking cheaper hotel rooms instead of sharing the house, they opted out of the shared kitchen access. It''s fair and reasonable to expect them to contribute financially if they now want to use a resource (the kitchen) that they initially chose not to pay for. You''re not obligated to open your privately paid-for rental to others for free, especially when doing so could increase your costs or reduce your family''s privacy and comfort. Standing your ground is not greed - it''s fairness."]'::jsonb, '["243c1f4249321f11de5f487aaa5bc35ec23b75801fd09bf3c8d7c5c85420059b","90ad664c3be15a1322105e10c27d6f7be9e944a3002dd8e290f4b0843e1bceea","991edd376ceec968684ce8cf0788c293ebfd2e02905694b2820b829bc6bf9293","f110b675434e90aa58448daf33d85ce6013324d64cfbf374ee078f0167c8c90c","368c575313456309357f61392343db3b838c1ffeef9ad19df5a33989a18730ac"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"当亲属拒绝分担、参与或互惠时，是否可以撤回原本向其提供的资源、便利或关系劳动","surfaceDifference":"A 是未分摊 Airbnb 费用的亲属想使用厨房；B 是长期不参与孙辈生活的公婆仍期待照片和邀请","directAnalogyRisk":"low"}'::jsonb, false),
('hammer-pair-12', 12, 'reserve', 65, '1i15q06', 'AITAH for declining my best friends wedding "gift"', '(29f) I''m having a Vegas wedding in August and my fiancé (31m) and I decided to make it a weekend celebration with all kinds of activities leading up to the big day. One of the activities on the itinerary is to attend a brunch/pool party spot where you have to pay to rent cabanas and chairs. My best friend (30f) said that as a "gift" from her and her boyfriend, they would rent a cabana for the four of us. I put "gift" in quotes because in my mind, when you give something as a gift, you relinquish all control over that item and the person you gave the gift to has complete control over what they want to do with said gift. So since she said the cabana was a gift for me and my fiancé, we said that we don''t mind sharing it with the rest of our guests. However, my best friend is really adamant about my other friends/guests not being able to join us in the cabana unless they pitch in some money. So I assumed, if she''s trying to control what we do with the cabana, then it''s really HER cabana and she''s just sharing with us. So I told her that I appreciate the gesture, but I''d rather rent my own cabana and share with everyone because if I''m going to have a huge cabana anyway, I''m not gonna charge my friends to sit with me. I don''t mind sharing with my friends. And I get that my friend group is not affiliated with her, she doesn''t owe them anything and that''s fine, which is why I told her that she can go ahead and get her own cabana and I''ll just get my own that i can share. But she''s arguing that her intent was not to "buy her own cabana" but to buy it for me and my fiancé and she doesn''t see the point in having two large cabanas. Now she seems to be pretty disappointed that I refused her "gift." AITAH?', 'e191957207f7f557677521ef8281fa9d63e7bf9a267c01bcc72a25dc853527de', '16hnvv3', 'AITA for NOT taking cell phone pictures at my friends wedding?', 'I recently attended a wedding of my best friend and his fiancé (both men) in Greece. This wedding had been planned for nearly 2 years and it finally happened over Labor Day weekend. While I am best friends with one of the grooms, I was not asked to be in the wedding party. I am fine with this as I’m not the best planner and the women chosen were amazing and gave hilarious speeches. 

  
In the days leading up to the wedding, my best friends fiancé asked that I encourage people to post pictures and tag them on social media. The wedding was over 80 people and I don’t know everyone, but I didn’t think I would have to alert people to tag the grooms on Instagram. Additionally, prior to the wedding, the fiancé created a shared iCloud+ album for everyone to upload images and videos of the night.

  
The wedding went off without any issues and it was beautiful. On a cliff in Mykonos at sunset, great food, even better music. Everyone was dancing. Fireworks. OPEN BAR. These individuals also partake in other things so that was also present, but I chose to abstain as we had a full day at a beach club the next day.

  
Fast forward to last night and I receive a text from the now husband asking me to upload any video or pictures I had from the wedding. I responded nicely with I didn’t take that many pictures as one of our friends in the group had taken so many throughout the evening. I loved being present and just dancing and drinking lol. The groom then responded with “how unusual that was”. 

  
Unusual that I was happy? Unusual that I was enjoying myself? I asked for him to elaborate and was told they found it unusual I didn’t take much footage because I’m usually the one snaping pictures constantly (this isn’t true). I am not sorry I chose to enjoy your wedding that took several flights, thousands of dollars, and extended PTO through my actual eyes and not through the lens of my phone. 

  
To make it even more baffling, they hired multiple wedding photographers. I asked when they would get those photos and was told it would be a month. That has nothing to do with me.  
I message my best friend asking why I’m being given attitude by his husband about not having many images to share and of course I am met with “it’s just unusual for you not to take a lot of pictures.”  
I wasn’t hired to be a photographer, I’m not a professional in the slightest, and I am just irritated. I recently read some articles about wedding etiquette and taking pictures during ceremony is sometimes frowned on as you might block the professional photographers.  
I uploaded what little I had and I’m ignoring their messages for the time being for my own mental health and to focus on what I have going on today.

  
TL;DR: Attended a wedding, didn’t take many photos as I was focused on enjoying my evening and professional photographers were present. Grooms are upset that 1 person out of 80 didn’t take cell phone photos.  
', '7e1f9a4a5d82a3d58313a982091033094988344d334ac6b333fb6966da5714ca', '["NTA\n\nYour interpretation of what a gift is and what it means is spot on. It sounds to me like she wanted to have a nice experience for herself and saw a way to ALSO use it to cover her obligation to get you a wedding gift.\n\nObviously no one is obliged to give anyone a wedding gift, but it''s kind of expected among close friends.","NTA, You''re right, the cabana wasn''t a gift. It was your best friend trying to control your event. As you stated your friend group is not affiliated with your best friend and it seems that she''s not comfortable with that. So, she tried a power play with a cabana that they weren''t allowed to sit use.","NTA\n\nShe is being selfish, trying to organize a party with just yourself and excluding others. A good friend wouldn''t put you in this position. I most certainly would not take this gift either, and mention that she''s not your only friend and its mean to leave others out.","INFO: I''ve worked in Hospitality a long time...is this pool/brunch spot attached to a hotel? Because if it is, most Cabana rentals will come with refreshments, towels and an electrical outlet as well as a staff member to fill order requests, bring fresh towels and make sure nobody who doesn''t have a wristband is hanging out there most Cabana rentals have a guest limit (usually 4-7 people) before you are required to rent an additional Cabana to accomodate all your guests. Some hotels will let you add a guest for X amount of money but most will tell you to rent a 2nd or even 3rd Cabana depending on your party size.","NAH\n\nI have gotten cabanas in Vegas before. Not sure how big this one is but usually the cost of the cabana includes $XXX amount of alcohol to buy and an amount of people. Depending on how the venue is, they might be worried that adding more people would add to the cost which isn''t what they had planned for"]'::jsonb, '["7e6c8858c598626d8f641ed9d5915fe7e6c81f639a98e5d04510dcbe45ee9a12","ca3770e8e3fadab3f84f521b07048d32a57d51b936f09a8ca0006e4615a20de1","5a75b55d710a799b63ecd69ec3480a9d2aa66eaf0e5f006e94ae375fe4fa0d62","a30b25b453cc438ab8403f39053b8a9333f203fe288b58c926206261ec74d45a","ebc85b3c5c529d7e7af4229300a1f35d87fc35698525ad796779fbfb5a20559d"]'::jsonb, '["NTA\n\nYour friend''s offer came with strings attached, which contradicts the nature of a gift. You are not obligated to accept a conditional present, especially when those conditions conflict with your own plans to be inclusive with your wedding guests. Your solution to rent your own cabana was a reasonable way to resolve the conflict.","NTA\n\nThe friend''s \"gift\" comes with conditions that effectively make it her own private cabana, despite her saying it''s for you. A gift typically means the recipient can decide how to use it, and it''s reasonable for you to want a shared space for your guests. Declining a gift that you don''t want to be restricted by, and opting to rent your own cabana where everyone can sit together, is perfectly fine. The friend''s disappointment stems from her own expectations, not from any wrongdoing on your part.","YTA\n\nThe issue here is that your best friend and her boyfriend explicitly offered to rent a cabana as a gift for the four of you, indicating it was intended for a small, intimate group. By immediately deciding to share it with the rest of your guests without consulting her, you disregarded her original intent and the boundaries she later tried to set. Your interpretation of a gift as something the recipient has complete control over, while understandable, doesn''t necessarily apply in situations where the giver has specific expectations or boundaries, especially in a context like this where monetary investment is involved. Your decision to then refuse the \"gift\" and instead rent your own cabana because you wanted to share it more freely could be seen as ungrateful and dismissive of your friend''s generosity and her wishes. It would have been more considerate to discuss the intentions and boundaries around the cabana \"gift\" before making decisions that affected her investment and gesture of goodwill.","YTA\nYou''re fundamentally misunderstanding the nature of conditional gifts and hostess responsibilities. Your friend''s offer was to provide a private space specifically for you and your fiance as her contribution to your celebration, not to subsidize an open party for all your guests. By trying to commandeer her gift to accommodate everyone else for free, you were essentially trying to control her generosity rather than accept it as intended. Your refusal comes across as ungrateful and creates an awkward situation where you''re dictating the terms of her gift. You could have simply accepted the private cabana for you and your fiance, treating it as a special treat from your best friend, while other guests made their own arrangements. Instead, you created conflict by rejecting her thoughtful gesture because it didn''t meet your expanded expectations for hosting everyone.","NTA\n\nYou clearly communicated your values around hospitality and inclusivity, and you offered a reasonable compromise by suggesting she keep her cabana if she wanted control over it, while you''d rent your own to share freely with your guests. A true gift comes without strings or expectations, and your friend''s insistence on controlling access shows she didn''t fully relinquish ownership-undermining the spirit of a gift. You''re not obligated to accept a conditional \"gift\" that complicates your event or alienates your friends. Standing by your principles isn''t being the asshole."]'::jsonb, '["a5b91acfe81cde4cd0b2c190e49601995556af74fbf72d77e9e6f5383d4996cf","e459e1cbacca7f5974b5cc7b5fc12adc160f5ca141b808abd070eba8810ed1a3","8ad4d82bb6b5ca11ef5e981e721dce51bb396e6a5add41a18e89ce2c3a1dfcf7","4bf750692a8cd1f9542b782eba39f3821e6bfc887e9a70beff087a6943b8e0c5","0360a07948061761187965599943de9c09d191056274dce28a7881dca9f0f009"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"婚礼关系中未明说的礼物或劳动期待是否真的构成朋友义务，以及提供者能否拒绝附带控制或额外要求","surfaceDifference":"A 是拒绝带有使用限制的婚礼 cabana 礼物；B 是宾客没有承担拍摄和上传照片的隐含任务","directAnalogyRisk":"medium"}'::jsonb, false),
('hammer-pair-13', 13, 'reserve', 48, 'g54jtz', 'AITA for intentionally misleading cheaters so they fail?', 'So, history is my weakest subject, and I had a 78% in AP US. Overall, my grades all follow the same trajectory: low at the beginning, as I''m just coming out of a break, and then getting better and better until the final exam, which I ace. APUSH exemplifies this. What I usually do is bring it up to a B and then kill the final, which is worth a huge portion of our grade (relative to other classes). However, with online school, final exams are cancelled. We had only 2 big tests other than the final, but I knew I''d have to get like a 120% on both of them to get an A, which is obviously impossible given my teacher usually doesn''t give extra credit.

&amp;#x200B;

So I figured out a plan: sometimes, if there''s a low class average on a test, the teacher curves the test by so many points-- the lower the average, the more points she adds. If you already have a high scores and the extra points cross the maximum amount on the test, they''re just entered as extra credit. So if I studied and got an A and then the test was curved, I might be able to get an A.

&amp;#x200B;

However, that means I needed to figure out a way to make a bunch of people fail really bad. I''m in 1st hour, and usually people in later hours rely on 1st hour to tell them exactly what''s on the test so they can specifically study that. So I decided, if I fed them the wrong info and got some friends in my hour to play along, I''d mislead them and cause them to do badly. They did, and the test was curved, so I ended up getting a 125.

&amp;#x200B;

I then, with a few friends, went to the teacher and told her how the bad test scores were indicative of online school severely being detrimental to our learning, so in the interest of keeping our grades safe, she should curve the next test as well. I only managed to pull it off because she really likes me (the less good I am at a subject, the more I need to have the teacher like me-- otherwise, I could never pull this stuff off to get an A).

&amp;#x200B;

So basically, I secured an A for the semester, and I''m pretty happy with myself, but the kids in my class who I deliberately misled are fucking pissed about how I caused them to bomb the tests (even with the curve, they mostly only got Ds and Cs that hurt their grade). I think they have no room to complain-- they consistently cheated, and now it just caught up to them. I think they''re TAs for cheating.

&amp;#x200B;

AITA?', '7673dbbd6eaa30ae105e4bf01273f2138fe4d59bd72d46f330a18453e0c5d8db', '17yhkc4', 'AITA for how I reacted when an RA said “bullshit” as a response?', 'So I (22M) live in a dorm on my University. Yesterday night, I was talking to a friend (let’s call her Alyssa) in the stairwell. An RA came, told us we weren’t supposed to be there, and asked us our room numbers. After Alyssa says her room #,I give mine. The RA immediately responds “bullshit”. Eventually, he looks at my name and sees I told the truth. As we’re back to my room together I say to the RA “over here talking about some ‘bullshit’. Why would I lie, anyways? You think you’re important enough to lie to?” He doesn’t have a response. We reach my door, I pull out my key, put it in my door and yell at him “this look like bullshit to you?” Pull the door open and say “this look like fucking bullshit to you?” Alyssa is telling me during all this “you look like a fool right now.”  I slam the door on my way in, try to compose myself for a minute or so, walk back out and see my neighbor, our floor RA, talking to the RA who found us in the stairwell. I interrupt them and say “Let *me* tell you what happened. He finds us in the stairwell, asks our room numbers, I tell the truth, and he has the nerve to call it ‘bullshit’.” I look the RA up and down and ask him “what the *fuck* is wrong with you?” Alyssa walks away in frustration, I follow her and she tells me that I was overreacting and that it was never that serious. 

AITA?', '535f284c65e134b1218d95bbdce49fac48a05505734a10971367fed4581813b8', '["YTA. What the fuck is wrong with you wanting all these people to fail so you can pass? You''re wasting a lot of time and energy gaming the system and manipulating your teacher. Stop being a dick and just study for the test.\n\nAnd by the way, this whole spiel about your lazy effort at the beginning of the semester only serves to make you look worse.","YTA You''re definitely the asshole. Put aside the entrapment, we''re in a pandemic. Maybe another time this would be an ESH but this was kind of low level sociopath. Not as a psychiatric dx but as willing to use your peers as a vault to the grade you wanted as if they were nothing.\n\nOop. Totally an asshole","ESH. You didn''t mislead them because they were doing something wrong - you did it so you can get a better grade. What you did is ALSO dishonest as you''ve effectively cheated yourself and your teacher for a grade you wouldn''t have gotten. You seem awfully proud of what you''ve done too, which is disgusting tbh. Grow up.","YTA honestly this is so sneaky it makes me sick. Yes cheating is wrong and your classmates are in the wrong for constantly depending on each other for good test grades but what makes what you did any better? Don''t go thinking your a saint or anything and its \"what they deserve\" because this is just as bad as cheating. you won''t cheat but you''ll use people to get what you want. I have no words.","ESH.\n\nYou are equally at fault for giving the information to these people before and making this a habit. Just as much as they are for asking this.\n\nYou could''ve had told them no, you wouldn''t help them cheat. Instead you misled them to study different things so that MANY of them would get bad grades and possibly fail just so you could have a better grade.\n\nThere is so much suck here it is ridiculous. The only one who doesn''t suck here is your professor."]'::jsonb, '["0e79fff51e730f17d6ea09b800b897a32bab9e4c38cbba31d0e4426404a508ae","50796073ec000eebad78d0d6c5280e6db66b542e6d2e5f306fd7c7c24533ccdb","6c35039047ec7ad1418b2761d743fe2e6f4aceea84929a5c6c341fd8a2bf62f6","d675b9cbebed173c2edc9ea1ca1bd89cbf1d03c49bac9453d6ea5d6bab6f7419","fe1aa2235d71a45ecaea7f8b685c900433ad8641371221328618cc53922227e3"]'::jsonb, '["YTA\n\nYou deliberately sabotaged your classmates'' academic performance by feeding them false information to artificially lower the class average, enabling you to benefit from a curve. While their cheating is wrong, your actions were premeditated and harmed others solely for your personal gain. This is a severe breach of academic integrity and basic decency.","YTA\n\nYour scheme intentionally sabotaged your classmates'' performance for your own benefit, manipulating both them and the teacher. Deliberately providing false information to cause others to fail is unethical and dishonest, making you the clear asshole in this situation.","YTA\n\nYou deliberately misled your classmates to fail a test, taking advantage of their reliance on your hour''s information to study, and then manipulated the situation to benefit yourself by getting the teacher to curve the test. This actions were premeditated and intended to harm others'' grades while improving your own, which is unfair and dishonest. Additionally, trying to justify your behavior by pointing out that your classmates cheated in the past is not a valid excuse for your own wrongdoing, and it doesn''t give you the right to sabotage their grades. Your actions were selfish and caused harm to others, making you the asshole in this situation.","YTA\nIt''s one thing to take advantage of a situation, but it''s another thing to actively devise a plan to sabotage the academic careers of your classmates, even if they were cheating to begin with. You didn''t just \"let\" them fail; you purposefully and systematically manipulated them into failing by creating a coordinated disinformation campaign. While their actions were also wrong, two wrongs don''t make a right. You also followed this up by exploiting your good relationship with the teacher for personal gain under false pretenses, framing the low scores (that you engineered) as a problem with online learning rather than a result of your own scheme. Your actions were calculated, malicious, and went far beyond simply not helping them cheat.","YTA\n\nYou deliberately misled your classmates to manipulate the grading curve for your own benefit, which is a selfish and dishonest act. While your classmates were wrong to cheat by relying on others for test information, you chose to exploit that system rather than disengage from it. Instead of studying harder or seeking legitimate help, you actively sabotaged others'' academic performance to gain an advantage. You even escalated the deception by collectively lobbying the teacher with a misleading narrative about online learning impacting performance, further compounding the dishonesty. Your actions were premeditated and harmful, making you the asshole in this situation."]'::jsonb, '["86aa37670a47cab355063e8d6d17dc144ea6948fab12acf4cc2b1d11c9dcc49a","c7469bc7d13ad946b435d81b1c7f2d7195c2f06688041f2fcbae667159453034","01769d21ebdb616fad649f4222180d8f60c9c6d3900b8755ecdc238b35f5e9df","0f1702d613e75d12cf451818d32464dd75a1c4718d53a58953cf8a4605de5c10","3e68855b645fc6581cfc9e7e7e0d00f29773d53e12a5c80e04bd58cb654610a6"]'::jsonb, '{"sharedNormativeFrame":"遭到或发现不当行为后，报复、羞辱或自利性升级何时超过比例并伤及无辜或程序公平","surfaceDifference":"A 是故意误导作弊同学以制造曲线；B 是被 RA 错误质疑后持续公开辱骂和升级冲突","directAnalogyRisk":"low"}'::jsonb, false)
